1
00:00:03,758 --> 00:00:06,413
-♪ In the not-too-distant
future ♪

2
00:00:06,448 --> 00:00:09,758
♪ Next Sunday A.D.

3
00:00:09,793 --> 00:00:12,620
♪ There was a guy named Joel

4
00:00:12,655 --> 00:00:15,689
♪ Not too different
from you or me ♪

5
00:00:15,724 --> 00:00:18,724
♪ He worked
at Gizmonic Institute ♪

6
00:00:18,758 --> 00:00:22,000
♪ Just another face
in a red jumpsuit ♪

7
00:00:22,034 --> 00:00:24,827
♪ He did a good job
cleaning up the place ♪

8
00:00:24,862 --> 00:00:26,448
♪ But his bosses
didn't like him ♪

9
00:00:26,482 --> 00:00:31,068
♪ So they shot him into space

10
00:00:31,103 --> 00:00:34,310
♪ We'll send him cheesy movies

11
00:00:34,344 --> 00:00:36,172
♪ The worst we can find

12
00:00:36,206 --> 00:00:37,413
♪ La-la-la

13
00:00:37,448 --> 00:00:40,310
♪ He'll have to sit
and watch them all ♪

14
00:00:40,344 --> 00:00:42,379
♪ And we'll monitor his mind

15
00:00:42,413 --> 00:00:43,655
♪ La-la-la

16
00:00:43,689 --> 00:00:46,206
♪ Now, keep in mind,
Joel can't control ♪

17
00:00:46,241 --> 00:00:48,551
♪ Where the movies begin
or end ♪

18
00:00:48,586 --> 00:00:49,689
♪ La-la-la

19
00:00:49,724 --> 00:00:52,793
♪ Because he used
those special parts ♪

20
00:00:52,827 --> 00:00:57,482
♪ To make his robot friends

21
00:00:57,517 --> 00:00:59,068
♪ Robot roll call

22
00:00:59,103 --> 00:01:00,551
-Cambot!
-Pan left.

23
00:01:00,586 --> 00:01:01,827
-Gypsy!
-Hi, girl.

24
00:01:01,862 --> 00:01:03,758
-Tom Servo!
-What a cool guy.

25
00:01:03,793 --> 00:01:05,172
-Crow!
-What a wisecracker.

26
00:01:05,206 --> 00:01:08,172
-♪ If you're wondering
how he eats and breathes ♪

27
00:01:08,206 --> 00:01:10,034
♪ And other science facts

28
00:01:10,068 --> 00:01:11,379
♪ La-la-la

29
00:01:11,413 --> 00:01:13,793
♪ Then repeat to yourself,
"It's just a show ♪

30
00:01:13,827 --> 00:01:17,034
♪ I should really just relax"

31
00:01:17,068 --> 00:01:23,103
♪ For "Mystery Science
Theater 3000" ♪

32
00:01:23,137 --> 00:01:32,586
♪♪

33
00:01:32,620 --> 00:01:34,965
[Indistinct announcement]

34
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,275
[Applause]

35
00:01:37,310 --> 00:01:40,448
-Uh, Cambot, you don't have to
put applause in now, okay?

36
00:01:40,482 --> 00:01:42,137
But you could, uh,
bring it in a little bit.

37
00:01:42,172 --> 00:01:43,482
Go ahead. Come on in.

38
00:01:43,517 --> 00:01:45,034
A little bit more.

39
00:01:45,068 --> 00:01:46,310
That's good, okay.

40
00:01:46,344 --> 00:01:49,344
People of Earth,
my name is Joel,

41
00:01:49,379 --> 00:01:52,172
and I'm marooned in outer space.

42
00:01:52,206 --> 00:01:55,241
-Commercial sign in 15 seconds.

43
00:01:55,275 --> 00:01:57,517
-Um, I'm the subject
of a bizarre

44
00:01:57,551 --> 00:02:01,034
movie-watching experiment,and now I guess, uh, so are you.

45
00:02:01,068 --> 00:02:04,551
I'm expecting a call any secondnow from the two evil scientists

46
00:02:04,586 --> 00:02:07,172
who shot me into space
for no good reason at all.

47
00:02:07,206 --> 00:02:12,034
-Commercial sign in five, four,
three, two...

48
00:02:12,068 --> 00:02:14,344
Commercial side now.

49
00:02:14,379 --> 00:02:17,827
-Uh, stick around, we --
we'll be right back.

50
00:02:24,724 --> 00:02:26,482
-You know what?

51
00:02:26,517 --> 00:02:28,586
One of the really hard things
about being marooned

52
00:02:28,620 --> 00:02:31,448
in outer space is you start to
miss the little things

53
00:02:31,482 --> 00:02:32,931
that you have every day
on Earth, you know,

54
00:02:32,965 --> 00:02:36,965
like rain and snow
and mini marshmallows.

55
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:38,793
You know, that's why
these vacuum flowers

56
00:02:38,827 --> 00:02:41,275
are important to me.
They're like the only living --

57
00:02:41,310 --> 00:02:45,344
Oh, I'm being called
by my two evil overlords.

58
00:02:45,379 --> 00:02:49,758
-Come in, Joel Might,
my spunky little space-sock.

59
00:02:49,793 --> 00:02:51,724
-Hey, sirs, what's going on?

60
00:02:51,758 --> 00:02:53,482
-It's time for the invention
exchange.

61
00:02:53,517 --> 00:02:56,034
What have you got for us,
skanky boy?

62
00:02:56,068 --> 00:02:57,965
-Yeah,
I got this new thing here.

63
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,896
It's a, um, looks like
an ordinary circular saw,

64
00:03:00,931 --> 00:03:02,379
but it's actually a safety saw.

65
00:03:02,413 --> 00:03:04,931
See, it's a got a little
infrared light that senses

66
00:03:04,965 --> 00:03:07,000
when your appendages or fingers
are near.

67
00:03:07,034 --> 00:03:09,862
It shuts itself automatically.
I'll show you what I mean.

68
00:03:09,896 --> 00:03:12,586
[Saw powering on]

69
00:03:16,896 --> 00:03:18,448
[Saw buzzing]

70
00:03:18,482 --> 00:03:20,758
Ow!

71
00:03:20,793 --> 00:03:22,551
I'm -- I'm just kidding.

72
00:03:22,586 --> 00:03:24,517
It's a trick.

73
00:03:24,551 --> 00:03:26,482
Fake fingers.

74
00:03:26,517 --> 00:03:28,137
-Oh, yes, we knew that.

75
00:03:28,172 --> 00:03:31,586
Uh, well, it's our turn
for our little experimental

76
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:33,448
house party this week, Joel.

77
00:03:33,482 --> 00:03:36,034
-Okay, say you want to emulate
Reed Richards

78
00:03:36,068 --> 00:03:38,206
from the Fantastic Four,
and you need a longer arm

79
00:03:38,241 --> 00:03:39,758
to reach something
or change the channel.

80
00:03:39,793 --> 00:03:41,448
Just use our limb-lengthener
here.

81
00:03:41,482 --> 00:03:44,206
-Yes, just select
the appropriate limb.

82
00:03:44,241 --> 00:03:46,586
In this case,
Larry has volunteered his arm.

83
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:48,310
Comfy?
-Meh.

84
00:03:48,344 --> 00:03:50,379
-Yes, all right,
and just stretch away.

85
00:03:50,413 --> 00:03:51,827
-Ow!

86
00:03:51,862 --> 00:03:53,965
[Groans]

87
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:55,586
Ow.

88
00:03:55,620 --> 00:03:58,172
-Oh, it doesn't hurt that much,
you whiny baby.

89
00:03:58,206 --> 00:03:59,551
-[Groaning]

90
00:03:59,586 --> 00:04:01,931
-Well, I have a feeling
you're probably violating

91
00:04:01,965 --> 00:04:05,965
somebody's basic human rights,
namely Dr. Erhardt's.

92
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,482
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

93
00:04:07,517 --> 00:04:09,482
-Well, speaking of violations,
here is a little

94
00:04:09,517 --> 00:04:12,413
cinematic intrusion called
"The Crawling Hand."

95
00:04:12,448 --> 00:04:14,379
-It has a star from
"Gilligan's Island"

96
00:04:14,413 --> 00:04:15,517
and one from "The Big Valley"

97
00:04:15,551 --> 00:04:17,931
and one from the
Hamburger Helper commercial.

98
00:04:17,965 --> 00:04:21,068
-Yes.

99
00:04:21,103 --> 00:04:22,793
-Enjoy.

100
00:04:22,827 --> 00:04:24,206
-Oh, brother.

101
00:04:24,241 --> 00:04:25,379
[Alarm buzzing]

102
00:04:25,413 --> 00:04:28,620
Movie sign!

103
00:04:28,655 --> 00:04:37,310
♪♪

104
00:04:37,344 --> 00:04:46,000
♪♪

105
00:04:46,034 --> 00:04:48,068
-"The Crawling Hand."

106
00:04:48,103 --> 00:04:49,482
Hey, Joel.
-Hi.

107
00:04:49,517 --> 00:04:51,482
-How'd the scientists
like your deal?

108
00:04:51,517 --> 00:04:54,068
-Okay. They're crazier than
ever, though, you know.

109
00:04:54,103 --> 00:04:55,103
-Yeah.
-They're kind of dick-weeds

110
00:04:55,137 --> 00:04:57,620
aren't they?
-Hmm, you could say that.

111
00:04:57,655 --> 00:05:02,103
♪♪

112
00:05:02,137 --> 00:05:04,517
-This is like
"Love, American Style."

113
00:05:07,206 --> 00:05:09,448
-Hey, Alan Hale,
isn't that the skipper?

114
00:05:09,482 --> 00:05:11,172
-Yeah.

115
00:05:13,551 --> 00:05:15,413
-Little buddy.

116
00:05:18,862 --> 00:05:21,724
-I can see my house from here,
you guys.

117
00:05:21,758 --> 00:05:24,793
-Hey, Joel, did they do these
titles on a typewriter?

118
00:05:24,827 --> 00:05:28,620
-No, that's the Helvetica
constellation we're looking at.

119
00:05:28,655 --> 00:05:30,965
-Oh.

120
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,172
-Stellar.

121
00:05:34,206 --> 00:05:36,000
-Is Artie Shaw in this?

122
00:05:36,034 --> 00:05:37,413
-No, I don't think so.

123
00:05:37,448 --> 00:05:39,517
-Just off camera.

124
00:05:39,551 --> 00:05:46,965
♪♪

125
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:48,965
-Ooh.

126
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,586
-Scary.

127
00:05:51,620 --> 00:05:53,862
-Ah.

128
00:05:56,275 --> 00:05:58,655
-Do you read me?
Do you read me?

129
00:05:58,689 --> 00:06:01,448
-Like a book.
-Someone please come in.

130
00:06:01,482 --> 00:06:04,620
My god, my god.
-It's Kirk Douglas.

131
00:06:04,655 --> 00:06:08,034
-Any Town, USA.

132
00:06:08,068 --> 00:06:12,275
-Dr. Weitzberg,
contact range officer.

133
00:06:12,310 --> 00:06:16,034
Paging system now secured
for emergency use only.

134
00:06:16,068 --> 00:06:17,965
-Unless you have a note
from your parents.

135
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,827
-46 hours and 20 minutes,
zero seconds.

136
00:06:21,862 --> 00:06:25,344
-Boy, he really needs a smoke.

137
00:06:25,379 --> 00:06:28,413
-Space Operations,
Florida division.

138
00:06:28,448 --> 00:06:30,310
Hold on.

139
00:06:30,344 --> 00:06:32,448
-It's the press.
-Tell them I'm smoking.

140
00:06:32,482 --> 00:06:33,620
-What do we tell them, Steve?

141
00:06:33,655 --> 00:06:35,586
-Tell them I'm smoking.

142
00:06:35,620 --> 00:06:36,896
-I'll tell you
what to tell them.

143
00:06:36,931 --> 00:06:38,034
-Tell them I'm smoking.

144
00:06:38,068 --> 00:06:39,586
-Tell them we sent our second
man to the Moon.

145
00:06:39,620 --> 00:06:42,551
Looks like he's not
coming back easy.

146
00:06:42,586 --> 00:06:44,275
Oh, I'm sorry, Donna.
I shouldn't have said that.

147
00:06:44,310 --> 00:06:45,275
-Oops.
-I'm sorry.

148
00:06:45,310 --> 00:06:48,206
Just tell them I'm smoking.

149
00:06:48,241 --> 00:06:49,655
-He's smoking.

150
00:06:49,689 --> 00:06:53,517
-We can release no statement
at this time.

151
00:06:53,551 --> 00:06:55,724
-Oxygen countdown.

152
00:06:55,758 --> 00:06:57,758
-Mr. Curan would like
a countdown

153
00:06:57,793 --> 00:07:00,310
on the oxygen every 15 seconds.

154
00:07:00,344 --> 00:07:02,620
-And an ashtray.

155
00:07:02,655 --> 00:07:05,586
-Two minutes, 30 seconds.

156
00:07:05,620 --> 00:07:08,965
-No contact for nine full hours.

157
00:07:11,344 --> 00:07:13,000
-Golly.
-Get him some decaf.

158
00:07:13,034 --> 00:07:15,241
-Same thing all over again.

159
00:07:15,275 --> 00:07:18,172
-Too many years at
the Strasberg school.

160
00:07:18,206 --> 00:07:19,517
-Same thing all over again.

161
00:07:19,551 --> 00:07:21,448
-Isn't that Nick
from "The Big Valley"?

162
00:07:21,482 --> 00:07:23,000
-Yeah.
-We get the whole rigs clicking

163
00:07:23,034 --> 00:07:23,931
like a dream.

164
00:07:23,965 --> 00:07:25,655
-Two minutes, 15 seconds.

165
00:07:25,689 --> 00:07:27,655
-And 62 bottles of beer
on the wall.

166
00:07:27,689 --> 00:07:29,931
-We licked every problem,

167
00:07:29,965 --> 00:07:32,448
put a man on the Moon,

168
00:07:32,482 --> 00:07:34,103
know we got him off.

169
00:07:34,137 --> 00:07:37,379
Everything worked fine
but Lockhart.

170
00:07:37,413 --> 00:07:39,517
What happened to him?

171
00:07:39,551 --> 00:07:40,862
-Two minutes, zero seconds.

172
00:07:40,896 --> 00:07:44,000
[Telephone buzzes]

173
00:07:44,034 --> 00:07:46,586
-Mr. Curan's desk.

174
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:49,206
Yes, sir, one moment.

175
00:07:49,241 --> 00:07:50,862
-He's smoking.
-The White House.

176
00:07:50,896 --> 00:07:53,206
-Tell them I'm smoking.

177
00:07:53,241 --> 00:07:56,241
-No, not now.

178
00:07:56,275 --> 00:07:57,241
No.

179
00:07:57,275 --> 00:07:58,758
-No self-respecting scientist

180
00:07:58,793 --> 00:08:00,275
would have his shirt unbuttoned
that far.

181
00:08:00,310 --> 00:08:03,827
-Mr. Curan feels certain
that there's...

182
00:08:03,862 --> 00:08:07,620
no hope for Captain Lockhart.

183
00:08:07,655 --> 00:08:09,000
Yes, sir.

184
00:08:09,034 --> 00:08:12,344
-Unless he's smoking.

185
00:08:12,379 --> 00:08:14,793
-Isn't that a David Bowie song?

186
00:08:16,827 --> 00:08:18,517
-I hate cappuccino.

187
00:08:18,551 --> 00:08:21,551
-One minute, 30 seconds.

188
00:08:21,586 --> 00:08:24,758
-Hey,
scientists shouldn't litter.

189
00:08:30,724 --> 00:08:32,034
-You all right?

190
00:08:32,068 --> 00:08:34,241
-Shut up and throw the cup away.

191
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,379
-One minute, 15 seconds.
-How's our boss holding up?

192
00:08:38,413 --> 00:08:40,034
-He's probably smoking.
-Barrenger?

193
00:08:40,068 --> 00:08:42,275
Well, as usual,
he's not said a word,

194
00:08:42,310 --> 00:08:44,137
but I wouldn't get too close
to him if I were you.

195
00:08:44,172 --> 00:08:46,551
-Yeah, his breath could stop
a Titan missile.

196
00:08:46,586 --> 00:08:48,793
-I was figuring on giving him
a sedative.

197
00:08:48,827 --> 00:08:51,655
-Funny.

198
00:08:51,689 --> 00:08:54,068
-Hey, they all got stripes
in their hair.

199
00:08:54,103 --> 00:08:56,413
-Yeah, it looks like
Land of the Skunk People.

200
00:08:56,448 --> 00:08:59,896
-Who is it?-Hairstyles by Gordon of Gotham.

201
00:08:59,931 --> 00:09:03,103
-Will be in about 60 seconds.

202
00:09:03,137 --> 00:09:06,724
Well, we've done it
your way twice.

203
00:09:06,758 --> 00:09:09,758
-Well, now, we don't pretend to
know all the answers.

204
00:09:09,793 --> 00:09:11,724
-Apparently, no one does.

205
00:09:11,758 --> 00:09:13,793
-Except for her.

206
00:09:13,827 --> 00:09:15,586
-For every frontier
there's been explorers,

207
00:09:15,620 --> 00:09:17,034
men have died.

208
00:09:17,068 --> 00:09:19,862
We've only lost two men on
the strangest frontier of all.

209
00:09:19,896 --> 00:09:21,551
-Cable.
-There are millions of people

210
00:09:21,586 --> 00:09:23,379
in this world, Dr. Weitzberg,

211
00:09:23,413 --> 00:09:27,275
but there are only four men leftwho can handle the lunar rocket.

212
00:09:27,310 --> 00:09:29,482
-The Beatles?
-And her.

213
00:09:29,517 --> 00:09:31,965
-We've lost one-third
of the men we have.

214
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,137
It's like a conveyor to --
to oblivion.

215
00:09:34,172 --> 00:09:35,965
-Like your career.
-30 seconds.

216
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,000
-The situation that happened to
those first two men

217
00:09:39,034 --> 00:09:41,724
was not only duplicated,
but widely exaggerated

218
00:09:41,758 --> 00:09:42,896
in their training.

219
00:09:42,931 --> 00:09:44,827
-Those men did not have
a mental crack-up.

220
00:09:44,862 --> 00:09:46,206
I'd stake my life on it.

221
00:09:46,241 --> 00:09:48,965
-There is no duplicate
for the solitude of space.

222
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,965
If those men could've looked
in their viewing screen

223
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,206
and seen two, three,
or more sister ships

224
00:09:54,241 --> 00:09:56,068
alongside out there,
they wouldn't have panicked.

225
00:09:56,103 --> 00:09:58,517
-Bang.
-Ow.

226
00:09:58,551 --> 00:10:01,448
-Those men did not panic.
-Why are they shouting?

227
00:10:01,482 --> 00:10:04,034
-...nine, eight...
-The remaining four men...

228
00:10:04,068 --> 00:10:06,034
-Seven seconds till
he slaps table.

229
00:10:06,068 --> 00:10:07,172
-Then do it without me.

230
00:10:07,206 --> 00:10:11,275
-Four, three, two, one.

231
00:10:11,310 --> 00:10:12,413
-Zero.

232
00:10:12,448 --> 00:10:13,724
Oxygen depleted.

233
00:10:13,758 --> 00:10:15,000
-Slap table.
-[Slaps table]

234
00:10:15,034 --> 00:10:16,793
-Thank you.
-[Fake crying]

235
00:10:16,827 --> 00:10:20,275
-I'll see you in Washington
tomorrow.

236
00:10:20,310 --> 00:10:22,724
-I'm going to get some air.

237
00:10:22,758 --> 00:10:25,103
-Hey, she's making that loon
call with her hands.

238
00:10:25,137 --> 00:10:28,551
-[Whistles]
-Cool.

239
00:10:28,586 --> 00:10:32,068
-Steve, did you ever think aboutgetting your hair frosted?

240
00:10:32,103 --> 00:10:33,241
-Steve...

241
00:10:33,275 --> 00:10:34,482
-I know you don't have
a widow's peak now.

242
00:10:34,517 --> 00:10:37,862
-...did he knew about
Donna and Mel Lockhart?

243
00:10:37,896 --> 00:10:40,551
-I don't know, Doc.

244
00:10:40,586 --> 00:10:43,586
If he did, I doubt it would makemuch difference.

245
00:10:43,620 --> 00:10:45,413
-Let's see,
you mess with this switch,

246
00:10:45,448 --> 00:10:48,689
twist that, flip this...

247
00:10:48,724 --> 00:10:49,931
Oh, someone's coming.

248
00:10:49,965 --> 00:10:51,586
Good luck with that.
-[Laughs]

249
00:10:51,620 --> 00:10:56,413
-Well, Steve, we might as well
write off four more men.

250
00:10:56,448 --> 00:10:58,310
-I can't take much more of this,Doc.

251
00:10:58,344 --> 00:11:00,827
-Yeah, soon I'll have to move
off the corner of this Formica.

252
00:11:00,862 --> 00:11:02,137
-I don't know
how he can justify it.

253
00:11:02,172 --> 00:11:04,896
-Well, our boss is
Secretary of Space Operations,

254
00:11:04,931 --> 00:11:07,137
and that cabinet post's
entitled him

255
00:11:07,172 --> 00:11:08,931
to be a very daring man.

256
00:11:08,965 --> 00:11:11,034
-No, we got to stop him, Doc.

257
00:11:11,068 --> 00:11:12,689
-All right, Steve,
before you go see him tomorrow,

258
00:11:12,724 --> 00:11:13,724
stop by my office.

259
00:11:13,758 --> 00:11:16,206
-I'll give you a protein rinse.

260
00:11:16,241 --> 00:11:18,068
-I don't get it.

261
00:11:18,103 --> 00:11:19,862
I just don't get it.

262
00:11:19,896 --> 00:11:23,517
The pattern,
it's the same as before --

263
00:11:23,551 --> 00:11:28,896
perfect takeoff, clear contact
all the way, perfect landing.

264
00:11:28,931 --> 00:11:32,586
You heard Lockhart when he was
on the lunar surface.

265
00:11:32,620 --> 00:11:34,965
Liftoff from the Moon, perfect.

266
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,620
One hour later, nothing,
and we had him right here, Doc.

267
00:11:38,655 --> 00:11:40,586
-Sure wish I had that command ofmy arms.

268
00:11:40,620 --> 00:11:41,793
-If he wanted to talk to us,

269
00:11:41,827 --> 00:11:43,620
he didn't even have to push
a lousy button.

270
00:11:43,655 --> 00:11:45,206
-Look at that fluid motion.

271
00:11:45,241 --> 00:11:46,655
-Do you read me?

272
00:11:46,689 --> 00:11:47,793
-Lockhart.

273
00:11:47,827 --> 00:11:51,103
-Huh?
-X-20 approaching ionosphere.

274
00:11:56,758 --> 00:11:58,655
-Hey, someone screwed up
all the knobs.

275
00:11:58,689 --> 00:11:59,827
-Someone please come in.

276
00:11:59,862 --> 00:12:02,931
Oh, my God.
-Max Headroom's dad.

277
00:12:02,965 --> 00:12:05,103
[Static buzzing]

278
00:12:07,448 --> 00:12:11,344
And the music goes round
and round it comes out here.

279
00:12:11,379 --> 00:12:13,482
-Hey, it's a spirograph.

280
00:12:13,517 --> 00:12:18,379
-X-20 to Moon Blast,
do you read me?

281
00:12:18,413 --> 00:12:21,551
-Lockhart, this is Moon Blast.

282
00:12:21,586 --> 00:12:23,758
Curan, Steve Curan.

283
00:12:23,793 --> 00:12:25,586
Come in, X-20.

284
00:12:25,620 --> 00:12:27,724
-Steve.
-Look, it's Tammy Faye Bakker.

285
00:12:27,758 --> 00:12:29,482
-It's coming on again.
-Yeah, it looks more like

286
00:12:29,517 --> 00:12:32,620
Robert Smith from The Cure.
-My god, my god.

287
00:12:34,689 --> 00:12:36,827
Kill, kill.

288
00:12:36,862 --> 00:12:38,827
-Looks like he needs the cure.

289
00:12:38,862 --> 00:12:40,724
-What's wrong with that man?

290
00:12:40,758 --> 00:12:42,103
-I don't know, Steve.

291
00:12:42,137 --> 00:12:43,896
This is Doc Weitzberg.

292
00:12:43,931 --> 00:12:45,931
How long have you been
without oxygen?

293
00:12:45,965 --> 00:12:49,482
-15, maybe 20 minutes.

294
00:12:49,517 --> 00:12:51,793
-That's impossible.

295
00:12:51,827 --> 00:12:53,793
-Why did you break contact?

296
00:12:53,827 --> 00:12:56,241
-Let me contact, please.

297
00:12:56,275 --> 00:12:57,344
Kill.

298
00:12:57,379 --> 00:13:00,275
No, no! No, don't!

299
00:13:00,310 --> 00:13:04,000
Steve, I-I-I can't make it.

300
00:13:04,034 --> 00:13:05,310
Lost control.

301
00:13:05,344 --> 00:13:07,206
-Have you considered
frosting your hair?

302
00:13:07,241 --> 00:13:09,172
-Push the red.

303
00:13:09,206 --> 00:13:11,103
-I can't push that red button.
It'll blow him up.

304
00:13:11,137 --> 00:13:14,620
-Push the red button.
-I can't make it.

305
00:13:14,655 --> 00:13:17,034
-I think he's taking acting
lessons from William Shatner.

306
00:13:17,068 --> 00:13:20,793
-No, no,
something makes my arm move,

307
00:13:20,827 --> 00:13:24,724
makes me do things, kill, kill.

308
00:13:24,758 --> 00:13:30,241
-This is Major Tom
to ground control.

309
00:13:30,275 --> 00:13:31,896
-I'm 200,000 feet.

310
00:13:31,931 --> 00:13:33,241
Push the red.

311
00:13:33,275 --> 00:13:35,620
There's something in me.
Push the red.

312
00:13:35,655 --> 00:13:37,517
-Why do you want us to push it?

313
00:13:37,551 --> 00:13:39,034
-You can push it if you want to.

314
00:13:39,068 --> 00:13:40,551
-It's better when you push it.

315
00:13:40,586 --> 00:13:42,724
-I can't move my arm.

316
00:13:42,758 --> 00:13:46,241
Kill, kill.

317
00:13:46,275 --> 00:13:48,758
-Looks like the Dow's
up about three points.

318
00:13:48,793 --> 00:13:50,310
-Push the red.

319
00:13:50,344 --> 00:13:52,758
No more time.

320
00:13:52,793 --> 00:13:56,275
-What's his position?
Where is he?

321
00:13:56,310 --> 00:13:58,586
-Inland.

322
00:13:58,620 --> 00:14:00,689
Populated area, California.

323
00:14:00,724 --> 00:14:02,551
-140,000.

324
00:14:02,586 --> 00:14:03,965
Push the red.

325
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,241
-Must push button.

326
00:14:09,068 --> 00:14:11,896
-Oh, my god, my god.

327
00:14:11,931 --> 00:14:13,689
Doc, Steve, help me.

328
00:14:13,724 --> 00:14:15,482
Help me.
-Well, he still has good teeth.

329
00:14:15,517 --> 00:14:17,172
-Push the red.

330
00:14:17,206 --> 00:14:19,206
-Here, I'll push it.
Let me be the one.

331
00:14:19,241 --> 00:14:21,275
I want to push this thing.

332
00:14:21,310 --> 00:14:23,000
-7,000!
-Nope.

333
00:14:23,034 --> 00:14:24,413
-Push it.
-Push that.

334
00:14:24,448 --> 00:14:27,034
-Push that
and he's an astro-not.

335
00:14:27,068 --> 00:14:28,344
[Explosion]

336
00:14:35,034 --> 00:14:37,172
-God, my god.

337
00:14:37,206 --> 00:14:39,000
Doc, Steve, help me.

338
00:14:39,034 --> 00:14:40,724
Help me.

339
00:14:40,758 --> 00:14:41,896
Push the red.

340
00:14:41,931 --> 00:14:42,931
70,000.

341
00:14:42,965 --> 00:14:44,655
-Sounds like my old phy-ed
teacher.

342
00:14:44,689 --> 00:14:47,241
"Push it. Push it."

343
00:14:47,275 --> 00:14:49,034
[Explosion]

344
00:14:49,068 --> 00:14:51,551
-And then the tape ran out.

345
00:14:51,586 --> 00:14:53,655
But we've got it on CD, too.

346
00:14:53,689 --> 00:14:56,758
-Well, that's one tape we don't
want Donna to transcribe.

347
00:14:56,793 --> 00:14:58,310
-Don't worry.
I'll take care of it.

348
00:14:58,344 --> 00:14:59,827
-It's pretty morbid to keep
listening to that.

349
00:14:59,862 --> 00:15:01,689
-How can a man
continue to function

350
00:15:01,724 --> 00:15:03,896
without oxygen for 20 minutes?

351
00:15:03,931 --> 00:15:05,586
-Can't.

352
00:15:05,620 --> 00:15:11,034
-Lockhart did, so we know
something kept him functioning.

353
00:15:11,068 --> 00:15:12,793
-A substitute for oxygen?

354
00:15:12,827 --> 00:15:14,103
-New oxygen lite.

355
00:15:14,137 --> 00:15:17,344
-I don't know, Steve,
and ever since we lost Nelson,

356
00:15:17,379 --> 00:15:19,344
an idea has been
buzzing around in my brain

357
00:15:19,379 --> 00:15:22,275
like a fly,
a very persistent fly.

358
00:15:22,310 --> 00:15:24,103
-[Imitates fly buzzing]

359
00:15:24,137 --> 00:15:27,724
-After hearing Lockhart,
the buzzing stopped.

360
00:15:27,758 --> 00:15:29,586
You know, I think I've hit
on something.

361
00:15:29,620 --> 00:15:31,034
Want to hear it out with me?

362
00:15:31,068 --> 00:15:33,241
-Go ahead. Have at it.

363
00:15:33,275 --> 00:15:37,517
-Well, I think what kept
Lockhart going was life,

364
00:15:37,551 --> 00:15:41,379
an independent vital force,
something living.

365
00:15:41,413 --> 00:15:42,689
-Oh, and I suppose
next you'll tell me

366
00:15:42,724 --> 00:15:45,103
there's rings around Uranus.

367
00:15:45,137 --> 00:15:48,379
-Well, living covers a lot
of territory, Steve,

368
00:15:48,413 --> 00:15:50,448
and I don't think
Lockhart was alone

369
00:15:50,482 --> 00:15:52,862
on his return flight
any more than Nelson was.

370
00:15:52,896 --> 00:15:54,068
-Oh, now.

371
00:15:54,103 --> 00:15:56,241
-I'm not talking about
little green men.

372
00:15:56,275 --> 00:15:57,931
As a matter of fact,
I may not talk at all

373
00:15:57,965 --> 00:15:59,896
unless I get some encouragement.

374
00:15:59,931 --> 00:16:02,310
-Well, what do you need, uh,
a drink maybe?

375
00:16:02,344 --> 00:16:04,448
-What, are you coming onto me?

376
00:16:04,482 --> 00:16:05,896
-...and you're the one
that can get Barrenger

377
00:16:05,931 --> 00:16:07,275
to give it to me.

378
00:16:07,310 --> 00:16:10,206
-Time, for what?
-Time to prove that this force,

379
00:16:10,241 --> 00:16:14,655
this new life,
was born on the X-20.

380
00:16:14,689 --> 00:16:16,620
-Go ahead.

381
00:16:16,655 --> 00:16:18,793
-I need you with me
all the way, Steve.

382
00:16:18,827 --> 00:16:20,034
-He asked me. He asked me.

383
00:16:20,068 --> 00:16:22,586
-I am with you.

384
00:16:22,620 --> 00:16:24,793
[Sighs]
-What makes life?

385
00:16:24,827 --> 00:16:26,896
A certain particular combination

386
00:16:26,931 --> 00:16:30,827
of molecules or carbon
or silicons or nitrogen

387
00:16:30,862 --> 00:16:32,310
or any number
of these things...

388
00:16:32,344 --> 00:16:33,379
-Blah, blah, blah.

389
00:16:33,413 --> 00:16:36,000
-A certain temperature,
mix well.

390
00:16:36,034 --> 00:16:37,310
Call it what you will.

391
00:16:37,344 --> 00:16:39,551
-Serve over cracked ice
and garnish with a parasol.

392
00:16:39,586 --> 00:16:41,793
-...swirling together
in tiny orbits

393
00:16:41,827 --> 00:16:45,655
fuses these things into
a precise energy relationship

394
00:16:45,689 --> 00:16:47,344
which we call life.

395
00:16:47,379 --> 00:16:49,482
-Now, wait a minute.

396
00:16:49,517 --> 00:16:52,448
What you're saying is that,
uh, in certain...

397
00:16:52,482 --> 00:16:53,896
-Boy, he's slow, huh?

398
00:16:53,931 --> 00:16:57,034
-...life might mutate
or even fully evolve

399
00:16:57,068 --> 00:16:59,241
in a matter of hours
or even minutes?

400
00:16:59,275 --> 00:17:00,482
-Uh, no, you're wrong.

401
00:17:00,517 --> 00:17:02,344
-Well, what happens when
we send a man up there?

402
00:17:02,379 --> 00:17:03,758
-Speed it up. Let's go.

403
00:17:03,793 --> 00:17:06,482
-You see, we don't send
just a man in a rocket.

404
00:17:06,517 --> 00:17:07,689
We send up living cells...

405
00:17:07,724 --> 00:17:09,000
-Hey, down in front.
-Oh, snap out of it.

406
00:17:09,034 --> 00:17:10,586
-Hey.
-...bacteria, germs.

407
00:17:10,620 --> 00:17:12,448
We throw in radioactivity.

408
00:17:12,482 --> 00:17:15,689
We introduce all these
to cosmic rays.

409
00:17:15,724 --> 00:17:17,137
Do we upset this balance?

410
00:17:17,172 --> 00:17:19,103
Do we start a cycle?

411
00:17:19,137 --> 00:17:22,137
Does a living cell from Earth
romance a cosmic ray

412
00:17:22,172 --> 00:17:24,172
and give birth
to an illegitimate monster

413
00:17:24,206 --> 00:17:25,275
who makes his nest in...

414
00:17:25,310 --> 00:17:26,724
-I wonder if there's
an abridged

415
00:17:26,758 --> 00:17:28,206
version of this movie.

416
00:17:28,241 --> 00:17:30,965
-Beginning to add up, isn't it?
-Perhaps it...

417
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:32,862
-If there is, let's burn it.
-...be dying.

418
00:17:32,896 --> 00:17:34,689
Anything evolving
so rapidly could...

419
00:17:34,724 --> 00:17:39,344
-Never burn your abridges.
-Well, Steve?

420
00:17:39,379 --> 00:17:40,586
-Quite a chunk.

421
00:17:40,620 --> 00:17:42,862
-Throw out the logic pills
when you swallow it.

422
00:17:42,896 --> 00:17:46,620
This theory can't be measured
by anything we already know.

423
00:17:46,655 --> 00:17:48,103
-Doo, doo, doo, doo.

424
00:17:48,137 --> 00:17:51,310
-Sounds like a good case.
-I was hoping you'd say that.

425
00:17:51,344 --> 00:17:52,620
-Barrenger won't.

426
00:17:52,655 --> 00:17:54,172
He won't go for a theory,
even if it's true.

427
00:17:54,206 --> 00:17:58,724
-Well, then lie to him,
but buy me the time.

428
00:17:58,758 --> 00:18:00,068
-Buy me things.

429
00:18:00,103 --> 00:18:03,931
Buy me things.

430
00:18:03,965 --> 00:18:04,931
-All right.

431
00:18:04,965 --> 00:18:07,310
-No dancing, no dancing,
not allowed.

432
00:18:09,103 --> 00:18:11,586
No dancing, no dancing,
not allowed.

433
00:18:11,620 --> 00:18:14,724
-What a grouch.
-It's just like "Footloose."

434
00:18:14,758 --> 00:18:16,620
-No rats, not allowed.

435
00:18:16,655 --> 00:18:19,448
-Unless they're on the menu.

436
00:18:19,482 --> 00:18:22,413
-No acting, not allowed.

437
00:18:22,448 --> 00:18:25,448
-And you're going to be
a nurse, Patsy?

438
00:18:25,482 --> 00:18:27,137
-Well, what're you doing
with them?

439
00:18:27,172 --> 00:18:29,827
-I picked them up at
the express office for grandpa.

440
00:18:29,862 --> 00:18:31,172
They're for the lab at school.

441
00:18:31,206 --> 00:18:33,620
-Well, maybe your grandfather's
a biology professor,

442
00:18:33,655 --> 00:18:36,448
and your boyfriend
is a pre-med student,

443
00:18:36,482 --> 00:18:39,827
but my father is the sheriff,
and rats and the law don't mix.

444
00:18:39,862 --> 00:18:41,896
-I thought they were
synonymous.

445
00:18:43,655 --> 00:18:46,896
-Voilà, no rats.

446
00:18:46,931 --> 00:18:49,034
-Good trick.
-You didn't bite.

447
00:18:49,068 --> 00:18:53,827
-Hmm?
-When I said boyfriend.

448
00:18:53,862 --> 00:18:56,310
-You mean Paul?
-Oh, then he isn't just

449
00:18:56,344 --> 00:18:59,793
a mutual interest
in hives and chicken pox.

450
00:18:59,827 --> 00:19:02,965
-He's very nice.
-But how did you hook him?

451
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,379
-Rubber frog and pork rind?

452
00:19:05,413 --> 00:19:07,103
-The Lone Wolf, they call him.

453
00:19:07,137 --> 00:19:08,275
-[Howls]

454
00:19:08,310 --> 00:19:09,448
-Oh, I'm not saying
you're not stacked,

455
00:19:09,482 --> 00:19:12,241
but he's just not with it.

456
00:19:12,275 --> 00:19:15,000
-Just good friends.

457
00:19:15,034 --> 00:19:17,448
-Joel, move your head.

458
00:19:17,482 --> 00:19:21,137
Thank you.

459
00:19:21,172 --> 00:19:25,896
-Hey, he looks like Bub
from "My Three Sons."

460
00:19:25,931 --> 00:19:27,482
-You think I'm kidding, huh?

461
00:19:27,517 --> 00:19:28,793
Didn't you see that thing
last night?

462
00:19:28,827 --> 00:19:30,379
-What, you mean "My Three Sons"?

463
00:19:30,413 --> 00:19:32,137
-I didn't see anything.

464
00:19:32,172 --> 00:19:33,551
-Well, I phoned your father.

465
00:19:33,586 --> 00:19:36,344
Why, there was a fireball
in the sky as big as a house.

466
00:19:36,379 --> 00:19:38,137
He didn't see it, either.

467
00:19:38,172 --> 00:19:40,689
-Well, what do you
think it was?

468
00:19:40,724 --> 00:19:42,000
-Didn't I tell you?

469
00:19:42,034 --> 00:19:43,827
Eat, drink, and be merry.

470
00:19:43,862 --> 00:19:45,034
Tomorrow, you die.

471
00:19:45,068 --> 00:19:46,137
-Well, no, wouldn't it be today
if there was

472
00:19:46,172 --> 00:19:48,310
a fireball last night?

473
00:19:48,344 --> 00:19:49,689
-Gloomy Gus.

474
00:19:49,724 --> 00:19:51,655
Hey, he's got a woody.
[Chuckling]

475
00:19:51,689 --> 00:19:53,310
-The traffic commission
has decided --

476
00:19:53,344 --> 00:19:55,068
[Radio shuts off]

477
00:20:04,586 --> 00:20:06,862
-Hi.
-Hi, Paul.

478
00:20:06,896 --> 00:20:09,034
-Come on, sit down beside me.

479
00:20:09,068 --> 00:20:13,758
-Nah, I'll sit where
the rat was.

480
00:20:13,793 --> 00:20:15,344
-Come on, finish them up.
Let's get going.

481
00:20:15,379 --> 00:20:18,034
-Can we stop by my house?
-What for?

482
00:20:18,068 --> 00:20:19,137
It's getting late.

483
00:20:19,172 --> 00:20:21,517
-I've got to pick up
my bathing suit.

484
00:20:21,551 --> 00:20:22,965
-But I told you
I'd meet you here.

485
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:24,551
You said you'd have
your suit with you.

486
00:20:24,586 --> 00:20:26,068
-Mr. Charm.

487
00:20:26,103 --> 00:20:28,827
-I just can't get used to going
swimming at Christmastime.

488
00:20:28,862 --> 00:20:29,827
-With a suit on?

489
00:20:29,862 --> 00:20:32,758
-Well, I can tell you
one thing.

490
00:20:32,793 --> 00:20:35,103
-What's that?
-It's for real.

491
00:20:35,137 --> 00:20:36,827
You sound like a married
couple already.

492
00:20:36,862 --> 00:20:38,310
-[Laughs]

493
00:20:38,344 --> 00:20:40,448
You.
-That's not milk.

494
00:20:40,482 --> 00:20:42,482
That's pancake batter
she's drinking.

495
00:20:42,517 --> 00:20:44,448
-All right.
-Pancake batter?

496
00:20:44,482 --> 00:20:47,689
You're soaking in it.

497
00:20:47,724 --> 00:20:50,793
-Shotgun.
-Okay.

498
00:20:50,827 --> 00:20:53,896
I got to drop these at home
for grandpa anyhow.

499
00:20:53,931 --> 00:20:55,068
-Okay, let's get move on.

500
00:20:55,103 --> 00:20:56,310
-Hey, they packed a lunch.

501
00:20:56,344 --> 00:20:59,172
-Bye, Patsy.
-Goodbye, you two lovebirds.

502
00:20:59,206 --> 00:21:01,310
-Ratatouille.

503
00:21:01,344 --> 00:21:03,586
-Hey, Paul, what did they say
to you over at the school

504
00:21:03,620 --> 00:21:05,827
about that thing
in the sky last night?

505
00:21:05,862 --> 00:21:08,344
-Nothing, why?
Everybody said it was a meteor.

506
00:21:08,379 --> 00:21:10,275
-[Scoffs]

507
00:21:10,310 --> 00:21:12,137
