I guess I'll just go up
to my room, if you don't mind.

754
00:31:34,758 --> 00:31:36,068
Thank you.
-Goodnight.

755
00:31:36,103 --> 00:31:38,103
-Hey, come on, one shot.
I'll just wing you.

756
00:31:38,137 --> 00:31:40,275
It won't hurt that much.

757
00:31:44,344 --> 00:31:47,103
-I've got to have that hand.

758
00:31:47,137 --> 00:31:49,482
Can't eat. Can't sleep.

759
00:31:49,517 --> 00:31:51,862
All I do is think about it.

760
00:31:51,896 --> 00:31:56,034
Maybe if there's something --
Oh, no.

761
00:31:56,068 --> 00:31:57,793
-Man, with an extra hand
around the house,

762
00:31:57,827 --> 00:32:00,379
just think of the stuff
I could do.

763
00:32:03,758 --> 00:32:05,482
Just think.

764
00:32:05,517 --> 00:32:07,137
-Hmm.

765
00:32:11,965 --> 00:32:15,034
-Star light, star bright,

766
00:32:15,068 --> 00:32:18,310
first hand I see tonight.

767
00:32:18,344 --> 00:32:19,413
-[Chuckles]

768
00:32:19,448 --> 00:32:21,827
-I wish I may, I wish I might.

769
00:32:23,931 --> 00:32:27,068
-Little human hand, come here.

770
00:32:27,103 --> 00:32:30,931
Little human hand, come on.

771
00:32:30,965 --> 00:32:33,965
Little human hand, yes.

772
00:32:35,310 --> 00:32:37,000
-If you want to catch
a human hand,

773
00:32:37,034 --> 00:32:39,620
you got to think
like a human hand.

774
00:32:39,655 --> 00:32:45,689
♪♪

775
00:32:45,724 --> 00:32:47,862
-That's giant.

776
00:32:47,896 --> 00:32:49,862
That hand's unbelievably huge.

777
00:32:49,896 --> 00:32:53,413
-It's called foreshortening,
Joel.

778
00:32:53,448 --> 00:32:55,482
-Coochy, coochy, coo.

779
00:32:55,517 --> 00:32:57,413
Coochy, coochy, coo.

780
00:33:00,068 --> 00:33:01,034
[Chuckles]

781
00:33:01,068 --> 00:33:04,241
-You've got to hand it to him.

782
00:33:04,275 --> 00:33:05,862
-Went out on a limb
with that one.

783
00:33:05,896 --> 00:33:06,931
-Oh.
-It's a good thing he brought

784
00:33:06,965 --> 00:33:08,965
that freezer wrap along.

785
00:33:09,000 --> 00:33:09,931
-I don't think
it's freezer wrap.

786
00:33:09,965 --> 00:33:12,413
I think it's a handbag.
-Oh!

787
00:33:12,448 --> 00:33:14,551
-[Laughs]

788
00:33:14,586 --> 00:33:18,620
♪♪

789
00:33:18,655 --> 00:33:20,862
-Oh, that'll go great with
a little glass of milk later --

790
00:33:20,896 --> 00:33:24,689
a little honey-cured hand.

791
00:33:24,724 --> 00:33:26,689
Next to the hand cream
on the shelf.

792
00:33:26,724 --> 00:33:28,137
-Yes, Steve.

793
00:33:28,172 --> 00:33:29,620
It's positive.

794
00:33:29,655 --> 00:33:31,172
There is a reading.

795
00:33:31,206 --> 00:33:33,137
-Yes, you've proved
that they are rats.

796
00:33:33,172 --> 00:33:34,586
-I said there was
a positive reading.

797
00:33:34,620 --> 00:33:36,758
This may be all the evidence
we need to convince Barrenger.

798
00:33:36,793 --> 00:33:38,310
-Oh, really?
-Oh, these two guys.

799
00:33:38,344 --> 00:33:40,517
We're in for some serious
acting now.

800
00:33:40,551 --> 00:33:42,068
-Don't you understand
I'm giving you the --

801
00:33:42,103 --> 00:33:44,103
-It's too late, Doc.

802
00:33:44,137 --> 00:33:46,344
-What's that?
-It's my resignation.

803
00:33:46,379 --> 00:33:48,103
-Oh, you talked to him?
-Oh, no, not quite.

804
00:33:48,137 --> 00:33:49,379
He talked.

805
00:33:49,413 --> 00:33:50,827
He said either get in his wagon
or get out.

806
00:33:50,862 --> 00:33:52,310
Well, I'm getting out.
-Well, now, wait a minute.

807
00:33:52,344 --> 00:33:53,482
Tear it up.
You don't need it.

808
00:33:53,517 --> 00:33:55,206
This is the gauge
I've been telling you about.

809
00:33:55,241 --> 00:33:57,620
It measures the results
of cosmic rays

810
00:33:57,655 --> 00:33:59,758
or the presence
of something associated --

811
00:33:59,793 --> 00:34:02,965
-What is the something, Doc?!
-Well, I don't know...

812
00:34:03,000 --> 00:34:04,034
-It's a something.
-...though, I did make

813
00:34:04,068 --> 00:34:05,793
a very important discovery
about Elmer.

814
00:34:05,827 --> 00:34:07,448
-Has he got it?
-Oh --

815
00:34:07,482 --> 00:34:09,931
-Well, how did he get it?
-Probably at a nightclub.

816
00:34:09,965 --> 00:34:11,344
-Oh, I see.

817
00:34:11,379 --> 00:34:13,517
You're not aware of Elmer.

818
00:34:13,551 --> 00:34:15,034
He's a very distinguished rat.

819
00:34:15,068 --> 00:34:16,310
-Perhaps you're familiar
with his glue.

820
00:34:16,344 --> 00:34:19,413
-He was a passenger
on our last space program

821
00:34:19,448 --> 00:34:22,275
before we launched
our first manned rocket.

822
00:34:22,310 --> 00:34:24,655
In the beginning, we thought
he was completely unaffected.

823
00:34:24,689 --> 00:34:26,620
-But he wasn't.
-I think the doctor has been

824
00:34:26,655 --> 00:34:29,034
spending a little too much time
in the rat lab.

825
00:34:29,068 --> 00:34:32,241
-I don't know when he'll turnon the other rats and kill them.

826
00:34:32,275 --> 00:34:33,896
Now, he can unlock
an ordinary cage.

827
00:34:33,931 --> 00:34:36,827
That's why I had
that padlock put on there.

828
00:34:36,862 --> 00:34:40,000
Everyone else around hereseems to think that what he does

829
00:34:40,034 --> 00:34:41,379
is in the range

830
00:34:41,413 --> 00:34:45,413
of an exceptionalrat's capabilities, but I don't.

831
00:34:45,448 --> 00:34:47,482
I think Elmer has thoughts.

832
00:34:47,517 --> 00:34:50,448
-Naughty thoughts.

833
00:34:50,482 --> 00:34:51,793
-Human thoughts.

834
00:34:51,827 --> 00:34:52,793
-Like a human?

835
00:34:52,827 --> 00:34:55,517
-Better than human thoughts.

836
00:34:55,551 --> 00:34:57,827
-Like the something,

837
00:34:57,862 --> 00:35:00,103
the same something
that was in our pilots.

838
00:35:00,137 --> 00:35:02,379
-Well, Steve,
there's a possibility.

839
00:35:08,689 --> 00:35:13,137
♪♪

840
00:35:15,689 --> 00:35:18,172
[Bottles rattling]

841
00:35:18,206 --> 00:35:20,689
♪♪

842
00:35:20,724 --> 00:35:22,241
[Glass shatters]

843
00:35:22,275 --> 00:35:23,896
-W.C. Handy.

844
00:35:23,931 --> 00:35:27,448
[Dog barking in distance]

845
00:35:27,482 --> 00:35:29,793
-Hey, she's wearing
a drool pad.

846
00:35:33,275 --> 00:35:36,655
-And she's putting on
Dr. Spock's housecoat.

847
00:35:39,068 --> 00:35:41,586
-Doctor or mister?
-Mister.

848
00:35:41,620 --> 00:35:45,586
-Doctor mister?
-Mr. Mister.

849
00:35:45,620 --> 00:35:48,137
-Mister misdemeanor.

850
00:35:48,172 --> 00:35:50,482
-That's what you get when
you rob a Dairy Queen, right?

851
00:35:50,517 --> 00:35:53,413
-Wow, she's pretty quick
with that cold steel.

852
00:35:53,448 --> 00:35:55,344
-Nuts in her girdle.

853
00:35:55,379 --> 00:35:57,551
-Good thing she has a handgun.

854
00:35:57,586 --> 00:35:59,724
[Barking continues]

855
00:36:02,344 --> 00:36:03,896
-Cujo?

856
00:36:03,931 --> 00:36:05,586
-Hey, boy.

857
00:36:05,620 --> 00:36:07,413
-Hey, come on.
This isn't funny.

858
00:36:07,448 --> 00:36:10,275
-Guys? Guys?

859
00:36:14,206 --> 00:36:16,482
-Don't take that off.
Your chin'll explode.

860
00:36:20,068 --> 00:36:23,310
-Jackson, you been making
this noise out here?

861
00:36:23,344 --> 00:36:24,965
Come on. Get down.
You know better than that.

862
00:36:25,000 --> 00:36:27,965
[Jackson meowing]

863
00:36:29,896 --> 00:36:32,448
-Oh, sorry, Jackson.
Was that your head?

864
00:36:32,482 --> 00:36:35,482
-What is this doing out here?

865
00:36:35,517 --> 00:36:37,413
-Just kind of lying there.

866
00:36:40,724 --> 00:36:41,793
[Meowing continues]

867
00:36:41,827 --> 00:36:44,931
-Oh, what a mess.

868
00:36:44,965 --> 00:36:47,275
-A chocolate mess.
-That boy.

869
00:36:50,379 --> 00:36:52,965
[Meowing continues]

870
00:36:58,034 --> 00:37:00,620
He said he wasn't hungry,
and then he comes downstairs

871
00:37:00,655 --> 00:37:03,482
and knocks these jars
off of the shelf.

872
00:37:03,517 --> 00:37:05,448
-That proves he's hungry.

873
00:37:05,482 --> 00:37:08,172
-...my jelly,
I'll tell you that.

874
00:37:08,206 --> 00:37:10,724
-Oh, Helga, that tickles.

875
00:37:11,413 --> 00:37:16,724
♪♪

876
00:37:16,758 --> 00:37:18,896
-Hey, it's the title character.

877
00:37:20,827 --> 00:37:22,655
-Cleaning after this kid...

878
00:37:24,517 --> 00:37:26,413
-Mm-hmm.

879
00:37:26,448 --> 00:37:27,517
-I recognize him.

880
00:37:27,551 --> 00:37:29,172
He used to be with Def Leppard.

881
00:37:29,206 --> 00:37:31,586
-Jackson, get away
from that ectoplasm.

882
00:37:34,827 --> 00:37:36,379
-That boy.

883
00:37:36,413 --> 00:37:39,551
Wait till I get
ahold of him tomorrow.

884
00:37:39,586 --> 00:37:41,551
Why the shower curtain?

885
00:37:41,586 --> 00:37:43,275
-That's a good question
for anyone.

886
00:37:43,310 --> 00:37:52,068
♪♪

887
00:37:52,103 --> 00:37:56,344
-Ooh, someone better callMadge, the Palmolive caseworker.

888
00:38:01,310 --> 00:38:03,827
-Trying to wake me up
in the middle of the night,

889
00:38:03,862 --> 00:38:06,448
as if I haven't
had enough of it.

890
00:38:09,137 --> 00:38:10,103
Empty.

891
00:38:10,137 --> 00:38:12,068
-Goodnight, sweet troll.

892
00:38:12,103 --> 00:38:15,310
-She needs about 150 hours
of beauty sleep.

893
00:38:15,344 --> 00:38:20,482
♪♪

894
00:38:20,517 --> 00:38:25,689
♪♪

895
00:38:25,724 --> 00:38:28,241
-Aah, aah, aah!

896
00:38:28,275 --> 00:38:29,344
-Aah!
-Oh, no.

897
00:38:29,379 --> 00:38:30,655
Look out!

898
00:38:30,689 --> 00:38:34,241
[All exhale in relief]

899
00:38:34,275 --> 00:38:36,620
-They got us on that one.

900
00:38:36,655 --> 00:38:38,275
-I knew it was coming.

901
00:38:41,827 --> 00:38:44,931
-Can't believe she's got to do
that scene over again.

902
00:38:44,965 --> 00:38:51,482
♪♪

903
00:38:51,517 --> 00:38:53,689
Back in the kitchen.

904
00:38:53,724 --> 00:38:57,793
-She forgot to put her
chin hammock on.

905
00:38:57,827 --> 00:39:00,862
-Hey, what's that stuff?
-It's the nighttime coughing,

906
00:39:00,896 --> 00:39:02,413
aching,
sneezing, stuffy-head, fever,

907
00:39:02,448 --> 00:39:05,137
so you're being chased
by a human hand medicine.

908
00:39:05,172 --> 00:39:11,448
♪♪

909
00:39:14,241 --> 00:39:16,068
-Good.

910
00:39:19,517 --> 00:39:21,068
-You know things are bad

911
00:39:21,103 --> 00:39:23,724
when you keep a liquor cabinet
in your bedroom.

912
00:39:23,758 --> 00:39:25,827
-That ought to do it.

913
00:39:25,862 --> 00:39:28,000
-For a horse.

914
00:39:28,034 --> 00:39:35,931
♪♪

915
00:39:35,965 --> 00:39:38,517
-Oh, Helga. Oh, stewardesses.

916
00:39:38,551 --> 00:39:41,482
Me, you, chandelier swinging.

917
00:39:41,517 --> 00:39:50,206
♪♪

918
00:39:50,241 --> 00:39:52,827
-I can't believe she's going to
do this scene one more time.

919
00:39:52,862 --> 00:39:57,448
They used to call her One-Take
Sally, and now look, nothing.

920
00:39:57,482 --> 00:39:59,000
-Let's see.
If I look at the dictionary,

921
00:39:59,034 --> 00:40:01,448
I think
that's where I keep my stash.

922
00:40:03,103 --> 00:40:08,965
♪♪

923
00:40:09,000 --> 00:40:10,172
-Oh, I've seen this.

924
00:40:10,206 --> 00:40:12,896
This is when she does her
Judy Garland impression.

925
00:40:16,206 --> 00:40:17,827
-[Gagging]

926
00:40:17,862 --> 00:40:19,137
-What the?

927
00:40:19,172 --> 00:40:20,896
-Oh.
-No, I think she's doing

928
00:40:20,931 --> 00:40:23,344
Mrs. Captain Kirk.

929
00:40:23,379 --> 00:40:28,034
-Got to find push button.

930
00:40:28,068 --> 00:40:29,724
-Must lay down.

931
00:40:32,724 --> 00:40:36,586
Must perform death scene.

932
00:40:36,620 --> 00:40:38,034
-Oh, grab your pills.

933
00:40:38,068 --> 00:40:39,620
That'll help.

934
00:40:39,655 --> 00:40:43,793
♪♪

935
00:40:43,827 --> 00:40:45,827
-Helga?

936
00:40:49,068 --> 00:40:51,034
-Mrs. Hotchkiss?

937
00:40:51,068 --> 00:40:58,137
♪♪

938
00:40:58,172 --> 00:41:00,448
Mrs. Hotchkiss?

939
00:41:03,482 --> 00:41:06,482
-She's a victim
of a Hotchkiss disease.

940
00:41:10,379 --> 00:41:13,620
-I bet he's going to be all
choked up now that she's dead.

941
00:41:13,655 --> 00:41:15,310
All choked up,
get it, 'cause --

942
00:41:15,344 --> 00:41:18,931
-Mm-hmm.

943
00:41:18,965 --> 00:41:22,758
-And now a really big hand
for the little lady,

944
00:41:22,793 --> 00:41:25,103
Mrs. Hotchkiss,
right here on our very floor.

945
00:41:25,137 --> 00:41:27,724
-Hmm, rings, fingernails,
clippers,

946
00:41:27,758 --> 00:41:30,275
Lubriderm lotion, it can't be.

947
00:41:30,310 --> 00:41:32,655
No.

948
00:41:32,689 --> 00:41:36,827
♪♪

949
00:41:36,862 --> 00:41:38,448
It's a clue.

950
00:41:42,689 --> 00:41:45,689
-Hey, little hand, little hand.

951
00:41:45,724 --> 00:41:52,931
♪♪

952
00:41:52,965 --> 00:41:55,344
-A big old booger.

953
00:41:57,620 --> 00:42:00,448
-This goes in the jar
with the others.

954
00:42:00,482 --> 00:42:02,068
-Ew.

955
00:42:06,000 --> 00:42:09,275
-Operator, give me
the sheriff quick.

956
00:42:09,310 --> 00:42:11,034
-If he's not there,
I'll talk to the skipper.

957
00:42:11,068 --> 00:42:12,586
[Telephone ringing]

958
00:42:12,620 --> 00:42:13,896
[Electric razor buzzing]

959
00:42:13,931 --> 00:42:15,724
-♪ Shaving, shaving

960
00:42:15,758 --> 00:42:18,241
♪ Na, na, na, na, na, na, na

961
00:42:18,275 --> 00:42:20,344
-Who?

962
00:42:20,379 --> 00:42:22,793
Paul?

963
00:42:22,827 --> 00:42:25,137
Paul Lawrence?

964
00:42:25,172 --> 00:42:26,586
Will you stop talking so fast?

965
00:42:26,620 --> 00:42:29,310
I can't understand
a word you're saying.

966
00:42:29,344 --> 00:42:34,275
-[Panting] Dead, found her,
gunshot, and...

967
00:42:34,310 --> 00:42:37,310
-Hotchkiss.
-Yes, Mrs. Hotchkiss.

968
00:42:37,344 --> 00:42:39,758
-Now, don't touch anything.
-I won't. Just come.

969
00:42:39,793 --> 00:42:40,862
-I'll be there in five minutes.

970
00:42:40,896 --> 00:42:42,413
-Oh, hurry up. Oh, please.

971
00:42:42,448 --> 00:42:44,448
-Now, look, Paul.
I know how you feel, son.

972
00:42:44,482 --> 00:42:45,482
-No, you don't.

973
00:42:45,517 --> 00:42:46,965
-I'll get there as fast
as I can.

974
00:42:49,068 --> 00:42:50,103
-He doesn't know how he feels.

975
00:42:50,137 --> 00:42:51,379
He's never been attacked
by a hand.

976
00:42:51,413 --> 00:42:53,965
He has no idea how he feels.

977
00:42:54,000 --> 00:42:55,172
Look at that boy.

978
00:42:55,206 --> 00:42:59,068
He's got a ball of pain inside.

979
00:42:59,103 --> 00:43:03,034
-Try and raise the countycoroner, Earl, and call my wife.

980
00:43:03,068 --> 00:43:04,241
Tell her I'm liable
to up all night.

981
00:43:04,275 --> 00:43:05,689
-Oh, we'd better
get out of here.

982
00:43:05,724 --> 00:43:07,965
-I'll call his wife.
-Sure thing.

983
00:43:08,000 --> 00:43:10,862
-I'll tell her she's married
to a two-bit character actor.

984
00:43:13,827 --> 00:43:18,310
♪♪

985
00:43:20,862 --> 00:43:22,758
-[Grunting] Got to think.

986
00:43:22,793 --> 00:43:26,379
Must get back to Galileo Seven.

987
00:43:26,413 --> 00:43:30,103
Lives of 400 crew members
hang in balance.

988
00:43:30,137 --> 00:43:31,448
Aah!

989
00:43:31,482 --> 00:43:33,551
-Vision fading.

990
00:43:33,586 --> 00:43:36,379
Must --Must cut transponder from wrist.

991
00:43:36,413 --> 00:43:41,000
Fashion crude phaser device
with bed slat and lightbulb.

992
00:43:41,034 --> 00:43:42,827
-Must control myself.

993
00:43:42,862 --> 00:43:46,103
Must find pastel-colored
native female.

994
00:43:46,137 --> 00:43:48,448
Execute prime directive -- oh!

995
00:43:48,482 --> 00:43:52,137
-[Grunting]
Hands so young-looking.

996
00:43:52,172 --> 00:43:54,172
Mrs. Berg,
I thought you were dead!

997
00:43:54,206 --> 00:43:55,517
Oh, oh, oh!

998
00:43:55,551 --> 00:43:57,241
-Throat scratchy.

999
00:43:57,275 --> 00:44:01,103
Hands so dry,
can scratch name into it.

1000
00:44:02,310 --> 00:44:05,689
-Embarrassing cuticles
on fingernails.

1001
00:44:05,724 --> 00:44:08,482
Must utilize emotional memory
and get to...

1002
00:44:08,517 --> 00:44:10,241
-Listen, Servo, stop it.
Stop it.

1003
00:44:10,275 --> 00:44:11,413
-Stop?
-No, unh-unh.

1004
00:44:11,448 --> 00:44:12,724
-That was my big scene, Joel.

1005
00:44:12,758 --> 00:44:14,206
-Hey, yeah, we were
just getting into it.

1006
00:44:14,241 --> 00:44:15,379
-This just isn't working.

1007
00:44:15,413 --> 00:44:17,620
I don't know how Shatner
does it week after week.

1008
00:44:17,655 --> 00:44:20,068
-I don't know why he does it
or who lets him do it.

1009
00:44:20,103 --> 00:44:22,206
-I think we should just be glad
he does it.

1010
00:44:22,241 --> 00:44:25,034
[Alarm buzzing]
-Oh, we've got movie sign.

1011
00:44:25,068 --> 00:44:27,379
Let's go!
-Must get to theater.

1012
00:44:27,413 --> 00:44:35,206
♪♪

1013
00:44:41,310 --> 00:44:42,724
-That hand thing killed me.

1014
00:44:42,758 --> 00:44:46,137
It was hilarious.
-Yeah.

1015
00:44:46,172 --> 00:44:49,137
-Pretty funny.

1016
00:44:49,172 --> 00:44:51,689
-We have to play
more games like that.

1017
00:44:51,724 --> 00:44:53,517
I like role-playing games.

1018
00:44:58,172 --> 00:45:00,482
[Dog barking in distance]

1019
00:45:02,068 --> 00:45:04,620
-You know, this book
of lists has everything.

1020
00:45:04,655 --> 00:45:07,965
Lookit here -- What to do when arogue hand kills your landlady.

1021
00:45:08,000 --> 00:45:09,137
-Hmm.

1022
00:45:09,172 --> 00:45:11,448
-Roddy McDowall,
Milton Berle, huh?

1023
00:45:33,068 --> 00:45:35,275
-[High-pitched] Hello? Hello?

1024
00:45:35,310 --> 00:45:37,689
-Operator, I would like to make
a long-distance call, please.

1025
00:45:37,724 --> 00:45:39,655
-I'll do what I can,
but I can't promise anything.

1026
00:45:39,689 --> 00:45:41,413
You know how things are.
You know, we've really got to

1027
00:45:41,448 --> 00:45:44,034
wait till the light comes on andthen...

1028
00:45:47,620 --> 00:45:48,793
-Sheriff.

1029
00:45:48,827 --> 00:45:50,172
-Skipper!

1030
00:45:50,206 --> 00:45:52,275
Where's the little body,
little buddy?

1031
00:45:57,275 --> 00:45:59,137
-I-I heard a shot.

1032
00:45:59,172 --> 00:46:01,482
I ran down from my room,
and I found her like this.

1033
00:46:03,379 --> 00:46:04,896
-No signs of a bullet.

1034
00:46:04,931 --> 00:46:06,793
Did you hear anything else,
like a door closing

1035
00:46:06,827 --> 00:46:07,793
or somebody running?

1036
00:46:07,827 --> 00:46:09,172
-Or the sound of
one hand clapping?

1037
00:46:09,206 --> 00:46:12,689
-No, nothing.

1038
00:46:12,724 --> 00:46:13,896
-Looks like she's
been strangled.

1039
00:46:13,931 --> 00:46:15,448
You can see the marks
on her throat.

1040
00:46:15,482 --> 00:46:18,310
No doubt about it.
-Hmm.

1041
00:46:18,344 --> 00:46:21,655
-Hmm, this lamp
has panties on it.

1042
00:46:21,689 --> 00:46:22,965
-Now, tell me, boy.

1043
00:46:23,000 --> 00:46:25,241
Did you ever see
this gun before?

1044
00:46:25,275 --> 00:46:27,655
-It belongs to Mr. Hotchkiss.

1045
00:46:27,689 --> 00:46:32,000
She told me
that it had a hair trigger.

1046
00:46:32,034 --> 00:46:33,206
-Smells like a hair trigger.
-Yes.

1047
00:46:33,241 --> 00:46:35,758
It probably fired
when it fell off the table.

1048
00:46:35,793 --> 00:46:37,172
Now, I know this hard
on you, boy,

1049
00:46:37,206 --> 00:46:41,310
but anything you can remember
will help, anything at all.

1050
00:46:41,344 --> 00:46:43,482
Now, do you recall,
did she leave any valuables

1051
00:46:43,517 --> 00:46:47,241
around the house,
any sizable amounts of money?

1052
00:46:47,275 --> 00:46:49,482
-Nothing that I found rifling
through her stuff.

1053
00:46:49,517 --> 00:46:51,793
-Not that I know.

1054
00:46:51,827 --> 00:46:53,448
-Well, that isn't
the way rumor has it.

1055
00:46:53,482 --> 00:46:55,482
That is, if you can believe
my wife, Edith,

1056
00:46:55,517 --> 00:46:57,551
and you can't always do that.

1057
00:46:57,586 --> 00:47:00,931
-So then rumor
wouldn't have it.

1058
00:47:00,965 --> 00:47:02,965
-That'll probably be Earl.

1059
00:47:03,000 --> 00:47:04,275
-I'll -- I'll get the door.

1060
00:47:04,310 --> 00:47:06,689
-Wow.

1061
00:47:06,724 --> 00:47:08,034
Psychic.

1062
00:47:10,586 --> 00:47:12,793
[All meowing]

1063
00:47:14,034 --> 00:47:15,310
-[Purrs]

1064
00:47:15,344 --> 00:47:17,206
[All yowling]

1065
00:47:19,517 --> 00:47:21,034
-That should do the trick.

1066
00:47:21,068 --> 00:47:22,448
At least that's what my wife,
Edith, says.

1067
00:47:22,482 --> 00:47:24,034
It usually does for her.

1068
00:47:24,068 --> 00:47:25,517
Two of those and a glass
of warm milk,

1069
00:47:25,551 --> 00:47:26,793
and she's out like a light.

1070
00:47:26,827 --> 00:47:28,275
-Make sure you drink
it like a raccoon.

1071
00:47:28,310 --> 00:47:31,000
-We'll go over the routine
questions in the morning.

1072
00:47:31,034 --> 00:47:33,551
Now, Paul, just one other thing.

1073
00:47:33,586 --> 00:47:35,517
Maybe you don't want to
stay here in the house.

1074
00:47:35,551 --> 00:47:36,793
I could drive you over
to my house,

1075
00:47:36,827 --> 00:47:38,413
and we'll find you
another place tomorrow.

1076
00:47:38,448 --> 00:47:40,206
-I've got a couple of hammocks
and a sailor's hat

1077
00:47:40,241 --> 00:47:42,137
I'd like you to wear.
-No, I'll be all right.

1078
00:47:42,172 --> 00:47:43,896
Don't worry about it.

1079
00:47:43,931 --> 00:47:45,896
-Soap and water will take
that ink off.

1080
00:47:45,931 --> 00:47:48,448
You better go upstairs
and try and get some sleep.

1081
00:47:52,965 --> 00:47:54,931
-Strange kid, dipping his hands
in ink like that.

1082
00:47:54,965 --> 00:47:57,517
How do you figure?
-All through downstairs, then.

1083
00:47:57,551 --> 00:47:58,965
-Think you got anything?

1084
00:47:59,000 --> 00:48:00,655
-Well, most of the prints
were pretty small.

1085
00:48:00,689 --> 00:48:01,965
They seemed to match the ones
that we got off

1086
00:48:02,000 --> 00:48:03,310
the gun and flashlight.

1087
00:48:03,344 --> 00:48:05,137
-Prince is pretty small.
-Yeah.

1088
00:48:05,172 --> 00:48:07,689
I think so,
but I can't tell you.

1089
00:48:07,724 --> 00:48:10,586
Oh, there was one that might be
interesting,

1090
00:48:10,620 --> 00:48:13,000
got it off the medallion
she was wearing.

1091
00:48:13,034 --> 00:48:15,655
-How soon can we get a report?
-Well, I'll telephoto a set

1092
00:48:15,689 --> 00:48:17,448
of these to Washington
and run a make on them here.

1093
00:48:17,482 --> 00:48:20,103
We ought to know something in,
oh, five or six hours.

1094
00:48:22,551 --> 00:48:25,379
What about the kid?

1095
00:48:25,413 --> 00:48:26,620
-Earl, I don't know.

1096
00:48:26,655 --> 00:48:28,758
I don't like to guess
on a case like this.

1097
00:48:28,793 --> 00:48:31,034
I just don't understand why
he wants to stay in the house.

1098
00:48:31,068 --> 00:48:32,413
I should think he'd want
to get as far away

1099
00:48:32,448 --> 00:48:34,413
from here as possible.
-Ah, he's a medical student.

1100
00:48:34,448 --> 00:48:35,965
He's used to being
around dead bodies.

1101
00:48:36,000 --> 00:48:38,413
-I suppose.
-Just like us.

1102
00:48:38,448 --> 00:48:39,586
-Now, come on.

1103
00:48:39,620 --> 00:48:41,137
We got a full night's work
ahead of us.

1104
00:48:41,172 --> 00:48:43,931
-Yeah, maybe if we're lucky,we'll get an hour of sleep, huh?

1105
00:48:43,965 --> 00:48:46,344
-Yeah, nice improv, ha-ha.

1106
00:48:46,379 --> 00:48:49,172
-Coconut cream pie,
just doesn't figure.

1107
00:48:49,206 --> 00:48:50,862
That cat is thirsty.

1108
00:48:53,275 --> 00:48:55,586
-His pants shrunk.

1109
00:48:55,620 --> 00:48:58,068
-Now, to catch a hand,

1110
00:48:58,103 --> 00:49:01,379
you've got to think like a hand.

1111
00:49:01,413 --> 00:49:04,344
I'll lay out
some press-on nails.

1112
00:49:04,379 --> 00:49:06,896
Yeah, that would work,
or maybe some lotion,

1113
00:49:06,931 --> 00:49:09,068
Lubriderm or...

1114
00:49:09,103 --> 00:49:11,034
Yeah, I know,
some Isotoner gloves.

1115
00:49:11,068 --> 00:49:12,482
That would work great.

1116
00:49:12,517 --> 00:49:15,413
-Oh, magic lamp,
help me find the hand.

1117
00:49:15,448 --> 00:49:17,275
-Dr. Weitzberg.

1118
00:49:17,310 --> 00:49:20,103
Weitzberg, Weitzberg.

1119
00:49:22,655 --> 00:49:24,758
[Rotary telephone spinning]

1120
00:49:24,793 --> 00:49:26,758
-[High-pitched]
Hello? Operator, hello?

1121
00:49:26,793 --> 00:49:28,137
-Operator,
long distance, please.

1122
00:49:28,172 --> 00:49:31,793
-Long distance to where?Where would you like to call to?

1123
00:49:31,827 --> 00:49:33,689
-I'd like to call...

1124
00:49:33,724 --> 00:49:36,241
-Spit it out.
-...a Dr. Max Weitzberg...

1125
00:49:36,275 --> 00:49:37,448
-Weitzberg,
how would you spell that?

1126
00:49:37,482 --> 00:49:39,103
-...in Washington D.C.
-Washington D.C.

1127
00:49:39,137 --> 00:49:41,034
-No, no, operator.
I-I don't have the number.

1128
00:49:41,068 --> 00:49:42,965
-Can you spell the name?
-This is an emergency.

1129
00:49:43,000 --> 00:49:45,413
-Is it an emergency?
-No, no. I'll hold on, okay?

1130
00:49:45,448 --> 00:49:47,551
-Could you hold on?

1131
00:49:47,586 --> 00:49:49,586
-Uh.
-How do you spell that name?

1132
00:49:49,620 --> 00:49:51,172
-No, uh, Weitzberg.

1133
00:49:51,206 --> 00:49:53,034
-Weitzberg? Weitzberg?

1134
00:49:53,068 --> 00:49:54,620
-W...
-W...

1135
00:49:54,655 --> 00:49:56,034
-...E...
-W, E.

1136
00:49:56,068 --> 00:49:58,448
-...I, T, Z...
-...I, T, Z, got you.

1137
00:49:58,482 --> 00:50:00,034
-...B...
-Keep trucking.

1138
00:50:00,068 --> 00:50:01,379
-...E...
-I can hear you,

1139
00:50:01,413 --> 00:50:03,586
but you're fading.

1140
00:50:03,620 --> 00:50:05,034
Are you on drugs?

1141
00:50:05,068 --> 00:50:06,413
Would you like
to call the drug hotline?

1142
00:50:06,448 --> 00:50:09,620
-No, G.
-Are you tripping?

1143
00:50:09,655 --> 00:50:11,137
-B, E.
-Uh, be on the lookout

1144
00:50:11,172 --> 00:50:12,482
for a crawling hand,

1145
00:50:12,517 --> 00:50:16,206
crawls up on the banister
and then drops on people.

1146
00:50:16,241 --> 00:50:18,103
Are you sure you can
hold on the line?

1147
00:50:18,137 --> 00:50:23,103
♪♪

1148
00:50:23,137 --> 00:50:24,689
I can't hear you.

1149
00:50:24,724 --> 00:50:28,310
Uh, are you -- Uh, uh.

1150
00:50:28,344 --> 00:50:32,068
It tends to drop on people rightwhen they are about to pass out.

1151
00:50:32,103 --> 00:50:35,103
Hello?

1152
00:50:35,137 --> 00:50:37,517
-Oh, geez!

1153
00:50:37,551 --> 00:50:40,344
-Wow!

1154
00:50:40,379 --> 00:50:42,448
This is action packed.

1155
00:50:42,482 --> 00:50:44,413
-Come on. Spit it out.

1156
00:50:44,448 --> 00:50:47,275
Cough it up.

1157
00:50:47,310 --> 00:50:49,482
[Siren wailing]

1158
00:50:49,517 --> 00:50:54,793
♪♪

1159
00:50:54,827 --> 00:50:58,517
-Uh, first word, uh,
sounds like, uh, smoke.

1160
00:50:58,551 --> 00:51:01,931
♪♪

1161
00:51:08,655 --> 00:51:10,137
♪♪

1162
00:51:10,172 --> 00:51:12,551
-Coochy, coochy, coochy,
coochy, coochy, coo.

1163
00:51:12,586 --> 00:51:19,103
-Play-Doh face, Play-Doh face.
-Coochy, coochy, coochy.

1164
00:51:19,137 --> 00:51:20,793
-Did he just give him
a wet willy?

1165
00:51:20,827 --> 00:51:23,344
-I think he did.

1166
00:51:23,379 --> 00:51:24,896
With his own saliva.

1167
00:51:24,931 --> 00:51:26,758
Pretty tricky.

1168
00:51:26,793 --> 00:51:28,551
-Well, I sure could use a beer.
-Who couldn't?

1169
00:51:28,586 --> 00:51:30,241
-Mm-hmm.
-But we can't stop now.

1170
00:51:30,275 --> 00:51:31,310
-Bet there's some
in the kitchen.

1171
00:51:31,344 --> 00:51:32,793
-Not me, not here.

1172
00:51:32,827 --> 00:51:33,931
-Me neither.

1173
00:51:33,965 --> 00:51:35,103
-I'm looking for
a nice, cool beer.

1174
00:51:35,137 --> 00:51:37,275
-Uh-huh, a big tall,
frosty one.

1175
00:51:37,310 --> 00:51:38,965
-...hundreds of miles
away by now.

1176
00:51:39,000 --> 00:51:41,310
-Just think of that long, tall,
foamy brew.

1177
00:51:41,344 --> 00:51:42,655
-Yowza!
-Boy, you can talk a snake

1178
00:51:42,689 --> 00:51:43,689
out of his skin.

1179
00:51:43,724 --> 00:51:45,206
-Yeah, yeah.
-Okay, take a look.

1180
00:51:45,241 --> 00:51:46,965
With my luck,
she won't even have any.

1181
00:51:47,000 --> 00:51:49,206
-Dames like her
always keep beer around.

1182
00:51:49,241 --> 00:51:50,551
-Always.

1183
00:51:50,586 --> 00:51:52,344
-I told you there would be beer
in the refrigerator.

1184
00:51:54,413 --> 00:51:56,137
-Holy -- Come here!

1185
00:51:56,172 --> 00:51:57,689
-What's the matter?

1186
00:51:57,724 --> 00:51:58,965
-It's a two-for-one special.

1187
00:51:59,000 --> 00:52:00,103
-I don't know,
but I'm getting out of here.

1188
00:52:00,137 --> 00:52:01,310
-It's a body, dummy.
-Wait a minute.

1189
00:52:01,344 --> 00:52:02,620
It might still be alive.

1190
00:52:02,655 --> 00:52:04,413
-No, that's not a dummy.
That's a body.

1191
00:52:04,448 --> 00:52:06,103
-Listen, buddy, we were supposedto collect the dame

1192
00:52:06,137 --> 00:52:08,034
and that's all.
This guy is on his own.

1193
00:52:08,068 --> 00:52:09,482
-He is alive.
Let's get him out of here.

1194
00:52:09,517 --> 00:52:11,448
-You know, it doesn't get
any better than this.

1195
00:52:11,482 --> 00:52:13,448
[Siren wailing]

1196
00:52:13,482 --> 00:52:15,931
You spend a hard day
loading bodies, and you've got

1197
00:52:15,965 --> 00:52:17,620
one more than you bargained for.

1198
00:52:17,655 --> 00:52:20,482
It's Miller time.

1199
00:52:20,517 --> 00:52:23,379
W-W-What?

1200
00:52:23,413 --> 00:52:25,482
I was making
a long-distance call

1201
00:52:25,517 --> 00:52:28,310
and then all I remember is...

1202
00:52:30,862 --> 00:52:32,206
I slept with that?

1203
00:52:32,241 --> 00:52:35,206
-Wow, he really must of had
his beer goggles on.

1204
00:52:35,241 --> 00:52:37,034
-[Screaming]

1205
00:52:37,068 --> 00:52:39,034
[Tires screech]

1206
00:52:42,344 --> 00:52:44,137
-Oh, he's high.

1207
00:52:47,310 --> 00:52:48,655
-There goes the deposit.

1208
00:52:48,689 --> 00:52:51,241
-This happens every time
you work with a live one.

1209
00:52:51,275 --> 00:52:53,655
-Of course I don't believe it.
Who could?

1210
00:52:53,689 --> 00:52:55,896
And don't think I'm going to letsome fantastic story

1211
00:52:55,931 --> 00:52:58,103
about a ghost,fingerprints or no fingerprints,

1212
00:52:58,137 --> 00:52:59,620
stop me from carrying
out this project.

1213
00:52:59,655 --> 00:53:01,689
-Oh, now, wait a minute.
-I'm not waiting for anything.

1214
00:53:01,724 --> 00:53:03,344
-Calm down, Bucky.

1215
00:53:03,379 --> 00:53:05,344
-I was given a goal
and a time limit,

1216
00:53:05,379 --> 00:53:07,034
and the time limit
has just about run out.

1217
00:53:07,068 --> 00:53:08,793
-Lee, I think we're forgetting
the facts of the situation.

1218
00:53:08,827 --> 00:53:10,206
I know it all may sound
rather trivial

1219
00:53:10,241 --> 00:53:11,862
with all of your problems here,
but the fact of the matter

1220
00:53:11,896 --> 00:53:14,275
remains that a murder
has been committed,

1221
00:53:14,310 --> 00:53:15,862
and the prime suspect
in this case

1222
00:53:15,896 --> 00:53:17,655
happens to be one of our men.

1223
00:53:17,689 --> 00:53:19,793
-This was no suicide.
It was murder.

1224
00:53:19,827 --> 00:53:22,862
-I gather from the attitude
of Mr. Meidel of security

1225
00:53:22,896 --> 00:53:24,689
that it won't be long
before he applied

1226
00:53:24,724 --> 00:53:26,172
real pressure right here.

1227
00:53:26,206 --> 00:53:28,896
We may find ourselves right in
the middle of an investigation.

1228
00:53:28,931 --> 00:53:31,241
-However, you do have
an alternative, Lee.

1229
00:53:31,275 --> 00:53:34,034
When Meidel calls, you can gain
time by simply stating

1230
00:53:34,068 --> 00:53:35,620
that Lockhart had
no authorization

1231
00:53:35,655 --> 00:53:37,551
to be in California,
and at the present time,

1232
00:53:37,586 --> 00:53:39,620
you haven't the slightest idea
where he is.

1233
00:53:39,655 --> 00:53:41,206
An absolutely true statement,

1234
00:53:41,241 --> 00:53:43,379
one which you can't
possibly be criticized for.

1235
00:53:43,413 --> 00:53:45,827
-Unless you flub your lines.
-Steve and I could fly to Palms

1236
00:53:45,862 --> 00:53:48,620
and investigate
this ghost story.

1237
00:53:48,655 --> 00:53:51,310
It may hold up the project
a couple of days,

1238
00:53:51,344 --> 00:53:54,517
practically nothing to lose.

1239
00:53:54,551 --> 00:53:57,517
-Now what do you say?
-"It's tempting."

1240
00:53:57,551 --> 00:54:03,103
[Phone ringing]

1241
00:54:03,137 --> 00:54:04,482
-Alright. You win.

1242
00:54:04,517 --> 00:54:05,862
Go on. Take off.

1243
00:54:05,896 --> 00:54:10,551
-"Ha-ha! Now we can go work
on those o-rings."

1244
00:54:10,586 --> 00:54:11,862
-Barrenger speaking.

1245
00:54:11,896 --> 00:54:14,965
-"Hello, there. Are you wearing
rubber underwear?"

1246
00:54:15,000 --> 00:54:16,586
-Yes, Mr. Meidel.

1247
00:54:16,620 --> 00:54:20,137
[Engine roars]

1248
00:54:21,931 --> 00:54:23,413
-"Bye."

1249
00:54:24,655 --> 00:54:33,034
-"Got to think, must think.Must find hand. Must comb hair."

1250
00:54:33,068 --> 00:54:37,827
"Must get Vitalis, rub into
scalp, get rid of flaking."

1251
00:54:39,172 --> 00:54:40,689
-Paul?
-"Little buddy."

1252
00:54:40,724 --> 00:54:42,413
-Sheriff.

1253
00:54:46,931 --> 00:54:48,517
-Are you alright?

1254
00:54:48,551 --> 00:54:50,965
-Sure.
-Where have you been?

1255
00:54:51,000 --> 00:54:54,931
-"Out by the lagoon."
-I just took a walk.

1256
00:54:54,965 --> 00:54:56,379
I woke up.

1257
00:54:56,413 --> 00:54:58,103
I guess this thing bothered me
a little more than I thought.

1258
00:54:58,137 --> 00:55:02,344
I-I just took a walk.
-But you're filthy dirty.

1259
00:55:02,379 --> 00:55:03,724
-"Yeah, and you're
stocky tubby."

1260
00:55:03,758 --> 00:55:04,758
-You're all sweaty.

1261
00:55:04,793 --> 00:55:07,379
You must have walked
awfully hard.

1262
00:55:07,413 --> 00:55:08,724
Now, look, Paul.

1263
00:55:08,758 --> 00:55:10,862
I'm here because an ambulance
driver called me.

1264
00:55:10,896 --> 00:55:12,758
He told me a strange
kind of a story.

1265
00:55:12,793 --> 00:55:14,103
Have you got anything
to tell me?

1266
00:55:14,137 --> 00:55:15,620
-"Just a strange kind
of a story."

1267
00:55:15,655 --> 00:55:17,379
-No, sir.
-Alright.

1268
00:55:17,413 --> 00:55:18,517
Well, I'll tell you
what I want you to do.

1269
00:55:18,551 --> 00:55:20,103
I want you to stick close
to the house.

1270
00:55:20,137 --> 00:55:21,827
Now, I wouldn't go out at all
if I were you.

1271
00:55:21,862 --> 00:55:23,724
I don't care what you do,
but stick close to the house.

1272
00:55:23,758 --> 00:55:25,068
Do you understand?
-Oh, why don't you just go

1273
00:55:25,103 --> 00:55:26,379
ahead and arrest me
and get it over with?

1274
00:55:26,413 --> 00:55:28,241
-There's no charge
against you, boy, not yet.

1275
00:55:28,275 --> 00:55:30,724
-"I'm not a boy. I'm a man."

1276
00:55:30,758 --> 00:55:32,931
-I suggest that you go up
and get some sleep.

1277
00:55:32,965 --> 00:55:34,724
You've had a hard night.

1278
00:55:34,758 --> 00:55:36,413
-"Hard day's night."

1279
00:55:40,000 --> 00:55:41,448
-"D'oh!"

1280
00:55:43,172 --> 00:55:45,103
-"Hi, welcome to your room."

1281
00:55:47,655 --> 00:55:49,931
-"He's a rebel without a hand."

1282
00:55:50,931 --> 00:55:53,655
[Thuds, shuts drawer]

1283
00:55:55,586 --> 00:55:57,241
[Bed squeaks]

1284
00:56:01,137 --> 00:56:03,689
[Engine starts in distance]

1285
00:56:03,724 --> 00:56:06,517
[Engine revs, departs]

1286
00:56:08,896 --> 00:56:12,827
-"Dear Kitty, by the time
you hear this, I'll be dead."

1287
00:56:12,862 --> 00:56:14,689
-"Good morning, Mr. Phelps."

1288
00:56:14,724 --> 00:56:19,551
-I'm telling this really
for you,

1289
00:56:19,586 --> 00:56:24,862
Professor Farnstrom and Marta.

1290
00:56:24,896 --> 00:56:26,896
-"I would now like to sing
'Cathy's Clown.'"

1291
00:56:26,931 --> 00:56:32,241
-I know that you will both
realize that it is the truth...

1292
00:56:32,275 --> 00:56:34,137
-"I have touched
the unclean thing."

1293
00:56:34,172 --> 00:56:35,620
-...that I am not
out of my mind.

1294
00:56:35,655 --> 00:56:40,000
♪♪

1295
00:56:40,034 --> 00:56:41,482
You were right, Professor.

1296
00:56:41,517 --> 00:56:46,000
-"Dogs canfly.
Cold fusion isimpossible."

1297
00:56:46,034 --> 00:56:48,275
-...keep things to myself.

1298
00:56:48,310 --> 00:56:50,862
♪♪

1299
00:56:50,896 --> 00:56:52,758
I-I-I tried to be
a lone wolf and...

1300
00:56:52,793 --> 00:56:55,517
-"Now, I'm a dead dog."

1301
00:56:55,551 --> 00:57:00,137
-...I guess I wanted
all the credit for myself.

1302
00:57:00,172 --> 00:57:06,620
Marta and I were swimming,
and up on the sand,

1303
00:57:06,655 --> 00:57:10,689
we found something
quite interesting.

1304
00:57:10,724 --> 00:57:13,034
It was an arm...
-"Rocks."

1305
00:57:13,068 --> 00:57:15,275
-...just an arm.

1306
00:57:15,310 --> 00:57:20,758
It had a strange covering.

1307
00:57:20,793 --> 00:57:28,482
I-I-I guess
right away it must have been...

1308
00:57:28,517 --> 00:57:32,448
-"Tripping."

1309
00:57:32,482 --> 00:57:37,000
"Remind me not to
drop acid after school."

1310
00:57:37,034 --> 00:57:40,758
♪♪

1311
00:57:40,793 --> 00:57:44,758
-"He's doing his
Pete Townshend impression."

1312
00:57:44,793 --> 00:57:46,379
"There it is."

1313
00:57:46,413 --> 00:57:49,344
"People try to put us down."

1314
00:57:49,379 --> 00:57:51,172
-"That's the close-and-play
phonograph."

1315
00:57:51,206 --> 00:57:55,172
-"Drop it, stops."
-"Close it."

1316
00:57:55,206 --> 00:57:57,310
-"Shatters."
-"Step on it."

1317
00:57:57,344 --> 00:57:58,931
-"Smashes."

1318
00:57:58,965 --> 00:58:04,586
♪♪

1319
00:58:04,620 --> 00:58:05,827
-"Hey, it's Timothy Hutton."

1320
00:58:05,862 --> 00:58:09,000
-"No, it looks more like
an Adam Ant to me."

1321
00:58:09,034 --> 00:58:11,448
-"Oh, maybe."

1322
00:58:11,482 --> 00:58:15,620
-"It's from a place called
'Uncharted Desert Isle,' huh."

1323
00:58:18,620 --> 00:58:20,344
-"Dear Skipper,
why haven't you sent help?"

1324
00:58:20,379 --> 00:58:22,000
"Signed, the Castaways."

1325
00:58:22,034 --> 00:58:23,551
-When did this thing
arrive, Earl?

1326
00:58:23,586 --> 00:58:24,965
-Why?
What is it?

1327
00:58:25,000 --> 00:58:26,758
-It's from Washington.

1328
00:58:26,793 --> 00:58:28,000
We asked for a confirmation

1329
00:58:28,034 --> 00:58:30,241
on those fingerprints
at the Hotchkiss house.

1330
00:58:30,275 --> 00:58:31,931
Well, they say they belong
to a guy

1331
00:58:31,965 --> 00:58:33,275
I've never heard of, Lockhart.

1332
00:58:33,310 --> 00:58:34,551
-Who?
-"June's boy?"

1333
00:58:34,586 --> 00:58:36,620
-Melvin Lockhart.
Do you know a Melvin Lockhart?

1334
00:58:36,655 --> 00:58:37,724
-"June's boy?"

1335
00:58:37,758 --> 00:58:38,793
-Anybody in this town
with that name?

1336
00:58:38,827 --> 00:58:40,000
-Never heard of one.
-"June's boy?"

1337
00:58:40,034 --> 00:58:41,620
-That wasn't the half of it.

1338
00:58:41,655 --> 00:58:44,103
They're moving in.
-Who's moving in?

1339
00:58:44,137 --> 00:58:45,793
-"June's boy."
-Washington.

1340
00:58:45,827 --> 00:58:47,275
-"George?
Grover?"

1341
00:58:47,310 --> 00:58:50,103
-Great Caesar's ghost.
-"Julius, Augustus?"

1342
00:58:50,137 --> 00:58:52,793
-You tell me why they want in
on a case like this?

1343
00:58:52,827 --> 00:58:54,137
-"Curiosity, money?"

1344
00:58:54,172 --> 00:58:57,517
-Widow lady gets herself
killed, a prime suspect,

1345
00:58:57,551 --> 00:59:00,862
the only possible suspectI've got right here in my hands.

1346
00:59:00,896 --> 00:59:02,241
-"Boo."
-"Boo."

1347
00:59:02,275 --> 00:59:05,103
-I was going to make an arrest
within a couple hours.

1348
00:59:05,137 --> 00:59:07,620
-Kid?
-Most certainly the kid.

1349
00:59:07,655 --> 00:59:09,172
You know the way
he's been acting.

1350
00:59:09,206 --> 00:59:10,724
-"Like us, bad."

1351
00:59:10,758 --> 00:59:11,896
-He's the only one that could
have possibly have done it.

1352
00:59:11,931 --> 00:59:13,241
Now they come up
with a man named Lockhart...

1353
00:59:13,275 --> 00:59:16,344
-"June's boy."
-...and they're taking over.

1354
00:59:16,379 --> 00:59:17,758
Earl, I'll tell you
what's happened.

1355
00:59:17,793 --> 00:59:19,517
They've got so many fingerprintsin Washington,

1356
00:59:19,551 --> 00:59:22,862
they've got them all mixed up.
-"[Chuckles]"

1357
00:59:22,896 --> 00:59:25,586
-Well, what's the use?
We can't buck them.

1358
00:59:25,620 --> 00:59:27,206
Not yet.
-"What?"

1359
00:59:27,241 --> 00:59:28,482
-"[Clears throat]"
-"[Whistles]"

1360
00:59:28,517 --> 00:59:30,827
-They also want to have us
put a guard on the house.

1361
00:59:30,862 --> 00:59:32,827
Nobody gets in.

1362
00:59:32,862 --> 00:59:34,551
-Want me to go over
and sit on the place?

1363
00:59:34,586 --> 00:59:36,137
-Yeah, you better.
-"Ought to bring a cushion."

1364
00:59:36,172 --> 00:59:39,275
-While you're there,
keep an eye on young Lawrence.

1365
00:59:39,310 --> 00:59:40,896
I suppose I should have
picked him up long before this,

1366
00:59:40,931 --> 00:59:42,517
but I just didn't want him
to have a record

1367
00:59:42,551 --> 00:59:43,724
until I was 100 percent sure.

1368
00:59:43,758 --> 00:59:45,137
-"He's such a sweet kid."

1369
00:59:45,172 --> 00:59:46,827
-And then after those
Washington boys

1370
00:59:46,862 --> 00:59:47,965
get through fooling around,

1371
00:59:48,000 --> 00:59:49,034
I'm afraid he's still
going to be it,

1372
00:59:49,068 --> 00:59:50,758
and I'd bet $100 on him.

1373
00:59:50,793 --> 00:59:52,482
-Okay.
I'll get right over there.

1374
00:59:52,517 --> 00:59:54,931
-Alright.

1375
00:59:58,103 --> 00:59:59,655
-"Damn Mary Anne."

1376
00:59:59,689 --> 01:00:03,965
♪♪

1377
01:00:08,655 --> 01:00:12,034
-"Clearasil.
Clearasil."

1378
01:00:12,068 --> 01:00:13,896
"Oxy. Oxy."

1379
01:00:16,275 --> 01:00:17,862
-Yes, what can I do for you?
-Afternoon, Sheriff.

1380
01:00:17,896 --> 01:00:19,275
I'm Steve Curan.

1381
01:00:19,310 --> 01:00:20,827
This is Doc Weitzberg,
and we're from Washington D.C.

1382
01:00:20,862 --> 01:00:22,034
-"We talk real fast."

1383
01:00:22,068 --> 01:00:24,137
-Well, you government boys
really work fast.

1384
01:00:24,172 --> 01:00:25,931
Got here in a hurry.
Let me get you some coffee.

1385
01:00:25,965 --> 01:00:28,379
-Well, thank you, no.
-Yeah, no thanks.

1386
01:00:28,413 --> 01:00:29,931
-So tell me.

1387
01:00:29,965 --> 01:00:31,344
Who is this man Lockhart?
-Wait a minute.

1388
01:00:31,379 --> 01:00:33,517
Let's not get started offon the wrong foot here, Sheriff.

1389
01:00:33,551 --> 01:00:37,034
-Yeah, I know.
You ask the questions, I don't.

1390
01:00:37,068 --> 01:00:38,379
-"Huh?"
-Well, you boys are from

1391
01:00:38,413 --> 01:00:40,827
the Bureau aren't you?
-Well, that's just it, Sheriff.

1392
01:00:40,862 --> 01:00:45,275
We're from Space Operations.
-Spa-Space Operations?

1393
01:00:45,310 --> 01:00:46,965
Oh, no.
Wait a minute, boys.

1394
01:00:47,000 --> 01:00:48,275
This is Palms, California.

1395
01:00:48,310 --> 01:00:51,068
Population 2,306.
-"We don't have space here."

1396
01:00:51,103 --> 01:00:52,034
-A woman gets
knocked off and...

1397
01:00:52,068 --> 01:00:53,310
-We know all about that,
Sheriff.

1398
01:00:53,344 --> 01:00:55,103
We just want to ask you
a few questions is all.

1399
01:00:55,137 --> 01:00:57,310
-About this very man Lockhart.

1400
01:00:57,344 --> 01:00:58,793
Has he been seen?
-Well, now I don't know

1401
01:00:58,827 --> 01:01:00,517
who you boys are
or why you're here.

1402
01:01:00,551 --> 01:01:02,137
As far as I'm concerned,
you've got no authority

1403
01:01:02,172 --> 01:01:03,379
at all to ask me questions.
-Now wait a minute.

1404
01:01:03,413 --> 01:01:04,827
We didn't come out here
to get pushed around.

1405
01:01:04,862 --> 01:01:06,103
-Now, Steve, just a minute.
Sheriff, as a matter of fact,

1406
01:01:06,137 --> 01:01:09,586
all we want is your permission
to go through the house.

1407
01:01:09,620 --> 01:01:11,000
-Nobody goes through the house.

1408
01:01:11,034 --> 01:01:13,344
Not until the boys
from the Bureau get here.

1409
01:01:13,379 --> 01:01:16,206
And I've got an armed guard
that says nobody does.

1410
01:01:16,241 --> 01:01:18,310
-"Yowza."

1411
01:01:18,344 --> 01:01:22,034
-"Oh, yeah, well,
we know Buzz Aldrin."

1412
01:01:22,068 --> 01:01:23,689
-"Fatty."

1413
01:01:27,517 --> 01:01:33,275
-Oh. Oh, uh, ah.
Oh, it's not so bad.

1414
01:01:33,310 --> 01:01:35,689
I don't really have acne anyway.I can go to that dance.

1415
01:01:35,724 --> 01:01:37,551
It's not a pimple.
It's my nose. [Chuckles]

1416
01:01:37,586 --> 01:01:41,620
What was I thinking of?

1417
01:01:41,655 --> 01:01:45,551
-"Hubcap. Duck."

1418
01:01:45,586 --> 01:01:46,758
-"Oh, okay."

1419
01:01:46,793 --> 01:01:48,620
-Oh, uh, we just talked
to the Sheriff.

1420
01:01:48,655 --> 01:01:49,862
-Yeah, he called me.

1421
01:01:49,896 --> 01:01:51,413
-Didn't change his mind,
did he?

1422
01:01:51,448 --> 01:01:52,793
-Nope.

1423
01:01:52,827 --> 01:01:56,172
-Well, uh, do you mind
if we just look around outside?

1424
01:01:56,206 --> 01:01:58,034
-Nothing to see,
but help yourself.

1425
01:02:01,103 --> 01:02:04,000
-Do you have any theories?
-Yeah, somebody killed her.

1426
01:02:04,034 --> 01:02:05,103
That's my theory.
-"Huh?"

1427
01:02:05,137 --> 01:02:08,862
-"Norman, get away
from the window, Norman."

1428
01:02:08,896 --> 01:02:11,241
-Well, Steve, I guess we might
as well be on our way.

1429
01:02:11,275 --> 01:02:12,620
-Hey, Doc.

1430
01:02:12,655 --> 01:02:15,655
You notice they don't
build houses like this anymore.

1431
01:02:15,689 --> 01:02:18,413
-"Kick sheriff.
Kick him."

1432
01:02:18,448 --> 01:02:22,827
-Oh, no they sure don't,
do they?

1433
01:02:22,862 --> 01:02:24,241
-No, they don't make houses
like this anymore.

1434
01:02:24,275 --> 01:02:27,344
Huh?

1435
01:02:27,379 --> 01:02:29,000
-Well, we'll see you
around, huh?

1436
01:02:29,034 --> 01:02:30,103
-See you later.
-Yeah.

1437
01:02:30,137 --> 01:02:31,655
-"Goodbye."

1438
01:02:34,724 --> 01:02:37,482
[Car starts]

1439
01:02:37,517 --> 01:02:39,586
[Engine revs]

1440
01:02:39,620 --> 01:02:41,344
-"Uh, your muffler."

1441
01:02:51,517 --> 01:02:55,275
-"Rapunzel, Rapunzel,
let down your hand."

1442
01:02:55,310 --> 01:02:57,862
-"I think they're going to do
a scene from 'Cyrano.'"

1443
01:02:58,482 --> 01:02:59,931
[Closes door quietly]

1444
01:02:59,965 --> 01:03:01,689
[Bird chirps]

1445
01:03:05,000 --> 01:03:06,586
-Whatever we've got to get
done, we better get done fast.

1446
01:03:06,620 --> 01:03:08,896
That cold fish could be
around here any second.

1447
01:03:08,931 --> 01:03:11,379
-"Hey, I think somebody just
called me a cold fish."

1448
01:03:11,413 --> 01:03:12,689
-"Why?"

1449
01:03:12,724 --> 01:03:13,827
-Did you distinguish whether
it was a man or a woman?

1450
01:03:13,862 --> 01:03:15,034
-No, it was a pretty
quick glance, Doc.

1451
01:03:15,068 --> 01:03:16,448
I'm not sure.

1452
01:03:16,482 --> 01:03:18,482
-Look, Steve.
-Huh?

1453
01:03:18,517 --> 01:03:20,931
-"Incoming! Duck!"
[Crack!]

1454
01:03:20,965 --> 01:03:22,000
-"Oh."

1455
01:03:24,241 --> 01:03:25,931
-Huh.
Well, the gate is over here.

1456
01:03:25,965 --> 01:03:27,551
Gate is over...

1457
01:03:27,586 --> 01:03:30,413
Hey, how did you get over there?

1458
01:03:30,448 --> 01:03:31,862
Uh, oh, geez, oh.

1459
01:03:31,896 --> 01:03:32,862
It's down there.

1460
01:03:32,896 --> 01:03:34,517
Could you get that for me?

1461
01:03:37,034 --> 01:03:41,000
-It's a note.
-"Come back tonight after dark.

1462
01:03:41,034 --> 01:03:42,862
The back door will be unlocked.

1463
01:03:42,896 --> 01:03:45,379
I have something important
to tell you."

1464
01:03:45,413 --> 01:03:49,241
-"Wear something sheer."
-No signature.

1465
01:03:49,275 --> 01:03:50,241
-Well, I didn't have
a date tonight.

1466
01:03:50,275 --> 01:03:51,965
Did you?

1467
01:03:52,000 --> 01:03:53,275
-Well, looks like we both
have now, isn't it?

1468
01:03:53,310 --> 01:03:54,896
-Hmm.

1469
01:03:54,931 --> 01:03:56,275
Let's see.
Something sheer.

1470
01:03:56,310 --> 01:03:57,724
How about that pink
chiffon number?

1471
01:03:57,758 --> 01:03:59,758
Oh, no, wait.
You're using that.

1472
01:03:59,793 --> 01:04:01,551
What about that taffeta,
that tea length?

1473
01:04:01,586 --> 01:04:03,896
You know, the one I wore
to the IGY convention?

1474
01:04:03,931 --> 01:04:06,034
You know, the night
I went home with Yuri Gagarin?

1475
01:04:06,068 --> 01:04:07,448
You remember.

1476
01:04:07,482 --> 01:04:10,000
[Car door closes,
engine starts]

1477
01:04:10,034 --> 01:04:14,586
-Hey.

1478
01:04:14,620 --> 01:04:15,862
-Hey!

1479
01:04:15,896 --> 01:04:18,827
-Hey, wait a minute.
Stop or I'll say, "Hey" again.

1480
01:04:21,172 --> 01:04:23,241
-"Ouch."

1481
01:04:27,172 --> 01:04:29,206
-"Do, do."

1482
01:04:29,241 --> 01:04:31,551
[Door latch clicks]
-Paul?

1483
01:04:31,586 --> 01:04:33,068
-"How'd she get in?"

1484
01:04:33,103 --> 01:04:35,620
-"She found the hole
in the security curtain."

1485
01:04:38,448 --> 01:04:41,448
[Footsteps]

1486
01:04:42,482 --> 01:04:44,758
-"Oh?"

1487
01:04:47,724 --> 01:04:49,517
-Paul.

1488
01:04:51,310 --> 01:04:54,275
-What are you doing here?
Get out.

1489
01:04:54,310 --> 01:04:57,206
-Well, are you alright?

1490
